WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.090 --> 00:00:07.630
Good afternoon, everyone.

00:00:07.630 --> 00:00:09.150
My name is Daniela Rus.

00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:10.950
I'm a roboticist
and the director

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:14.310
of MIT's Computer Science
and Artificial Intelligence

00:00:14.310 --> 00:00:16.980
Laboratory, or
CSAIL as we know it.

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:19.890
And also Deputy Dean of Research
in the Schwartzmann College

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:20.880
of Computing.

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:25.050
Welcome to today's installment
of Hot Topics in Computing.

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:28.020
Now, our guest today
needs no introduction.

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:31.530
But I'd like to give
him a short one anyway.

00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:36.000
Neil Degrasse Tyson is an
astrophysicist and arguably

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:39.120
the world's most well-known
science communicator.

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:42.960
For nearly 25 years, he
directed the Hayden Planetarium

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:45.570
at the American Museum
of Natural History,

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:48.750
while also writing
dozens of books

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:52.880
and hosting beloved TV shows
and podcasts, such as Cosmos,

00:00:52.880 --> 00:00:55.340
StarTalk, and Nova.

00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:58.740
Neil received his bachelor's
degree in Physics from Harvard

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:03.360
and his PhD in Astronomy
from Columbia University.

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:08.730
And in addition to his degrees
and his extraordinary presence

00:01:08.730 --> 00:01:12.570
in the media, Neil
received more than a dozen

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:16.050
honorary doctorates,
as well as many awards.

00:01:16.050 --> 00:01:18.030
And what I would like
to highlight today

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:21.210
is the NASA Distinguished
Public Service Medal,

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:23.790
which is the highest
award given by NASA

00:01:23.790 --> 00:01:26.940
to a non-government citizen.

00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:29.940
He's even got an
asteroid named after him.

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:33.450
Neil, I'm so delighted that you
made time in your busy schedule

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:37.080
to join us today, to continue
the conversation we started

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:40.500
in December, to talk not
just about the stars,

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:44.340
but also how astrophysics
relates to the computer science

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:45.360
world.

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:47.550
So Neil, welcome back virtually.

00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:48.850
Welcome to MIT.

00:01:48.850 --> 00:01:49.350
Yeah.

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:50.017
Well, thank you.

00:01:50.017 --> 00:01:51.850
Thanks for having me back.

00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:52.860
I love your audience.

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:57.870
It's always inquisitive, and
energetic, and with a verve

00:01:57.870 --> 00:02:01.540
for learning and
asking questions.

00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:02.895
And so I'm all in.

00:02:02.895 --> 00:02:04.290
I'm all in for all of that.

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:06.110
So let's move on.

00:02:06.110 --> 00:02:06.610
Sorry.

00:02:06.610 --> 00:02:07.693
I hope you're ready, Neil.

00:02:07.693 --> 00:02:11.070
Because back in December,
I had about 200 questions

00:02:11.070 --> 00:02:14.400
from our amazing students
left after our conversation.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:15.920
And that number has doubled.

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:17.110
So let's get started.

00:02:17.110 --> 00:02:17.610
OK.

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:18.240
Let's do it.

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:20.040
That's just a question
every 20 seconds.

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:21.660
That's not a problem.

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:24.150
OK.

00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:30.360
Let me start with some questions
around the rise of data and AI.

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:33.810
Do see potential in AI to
solve general questions

00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:38.130
about the universe or will
AI be used to crunch numbers

00:02:38.130 --> 00:02:39.550
and equations?

00:02:39.550 --> 00:02:40.140
Yeah.

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:46.510
So I don't see AI getting ahead
of us in our cosmic inquiries.

00:02:46.510 --> 00:02:50.430
Now, maybe that's just
because I don't want it to.

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:55.410
And so I accept that
bias that I may have.

00:02:55.410 --> 00:02:57.210
I think I recognize that bias.

00:02:57.210 --> 00:02:59.760
But so much of the
questions we ask

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:04.290
about the universe come about
by looking up and saying

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:08.370
to ourselves, hmm, that's odd.

00:03:08.370 --> 00:03:10.920
That doesn't match
what it should--

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:12.480
that's unusual.

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:18.120
Isaac Asimov, great author
and an science writer

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:21.960
over many decades, he
made the perceptive point

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:25.980
that true scientific
discovery, contrary to what

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:27.420
we think of with--

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:31.290
was it, not Aristotle,
but Archimedes-- contrary

00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:34.350
to what the legend
would have you believe,

00:03:34.350 --> 00:03:36.930
true scientific discovery
doesn't come from somebody

00:03:36.930 --> 00:03:39.150
saying Eureka!

00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:44.505
It comes from somebody
saying, hmm, that looks odd.

00:03:44.505 --> 00:03:45.930
[LAUGHTER]

00:03:45.930 --> 00:03:48.300
What's going on there?

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:52.290
So I guess, AI, if it's
a complete mapping of all

00:03:52.290 --> 00:03:56.160
that it is to be human, that
I suppose, in principle, you

00:03:56.160 --> 00:03:59.760
could inculcate it with
a sense of curiosity

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:04.110
so that it will explore things
it doesn't know and then

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:08.280
bring to bear on it what it
does know to try to solve it.

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:12.540
I don't see why that
couldn't be the case.

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:17.370
But I don't know that we
have good examples of AI--

00:04:17.370 --> 00:04:19.440
yes, we have AIs
finding solutions

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:21.209
to problems that
had eluded people.

00:04:21.209 --> 00:04:24.400
I knew that took place
in chess and in Go.

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:26.340
Even in something as
simple as juggling,

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:31.230
there were solutions
to these tasks

00:04:31.230 --> 00:04:34.050
that we've had for so
long, that they just

00:04:34.050 --> 00:04:35.440
found another solution to it.

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:39.060
But would the computer be
able to invent another game,

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:40.400
for example?

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:43.410
I'm still wondering
whether I can

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:47.880
get the full sort
of abject creativity

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:51.300
that we know humans
are capable of?

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:55.490
Is AI going to paint
this behind me?

00:04:55.490 --> 00:04:57.070
Is it going to come--

00:04:57.070 --> 00:05:01.940
will it even think to
paint something like that?

00:05:01.940 --> 00:05:03.650
So I don't know.

00:05:03.650 --> 00:05:07.130
And I think cosmic
exploration is so--

00:05:07.130 --> 00:05:09.120
every year, it's like
who ordered that?

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:09.620
What?

00:05:09.620 --> 00:05:11.900
Oh, two black holes colliding.

00:05:11.900 --> 00:05:13.460
Oh, let's try to measure that.

00:05:13.460 --> 00:05:14.000
OK.

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:15.410
Oh, the universe is expanding.

00:05:15.410 --> 00:05:17.030
Oh, the universe
is accelerating.

00:05:17.030 --> 00:05:18.947
Oh, the universe
had a beginning.

00:05:18.947 --> 00:05:20.280
Oh, there might be a multiverse.

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:23.940
I don't see AI anywhere
on that landscape.

00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:26.360
So I know it's your
whole bread and butter.

00:05:26.360 --> 00:05:30.960
But I'm thinking it'll be a
long while if ever before AI

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:34.010
is sitting right next to me,
scratching our head-- whatever

00:05:34.010 --> 00:05:36.170
the AI scratches--
to try to figure out

00:05:36.170 --> 00:05:39.300
a new question and a new problem
that sits in front of us.

00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:41.330
So for me, it's
the data crunching

00:05:41.330 --> 00:05:44.330
that happens once we've already
established the problem.

00:05:44.330 --> 00:05:48.080
And now we want to
calculate upon it.

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:50.870
Oh, in an ideal
world, if you could

00:05:50.870 --> 00:05:54.930
have anything out of an AI
or machine learning system,

00:05:54.930 --> 00:05:57.750
what would you like
as an astrophysicist?

00:05:57.750 --> 00:05:59.000
Oh.

00:05:59.000 --> 00:05:59.825
Huh.

00:05:59.825 --> 00:06:01.700
I was thinking what
would I want as a person?

00:06:01.700 --> 00:06:03.500
Let me answer that first.

00:06:03.500 --> 00:06:06.260
Yeah, I'm ready for
self-driving cars.

00:06:06.260 --> 00:06:07.070
I'm ready for that.

00:06:07.070 --> 00:06:08.270
Nice comment, Neil.

00:06:08.270 --> 00:06:09.320
I'm working on it.

00:06:09.320 --> 00:06:10.250
Are you, OK.

00:06:10.250 --> 00:06:10.940
Well, hurry up.

00:06:10.940 --> 00:06:12.140
OK, all right.

00:06:12.140 --> 00:06:14.050
I got two kids who
don't want to drive.

00:06:14.050 --> 00:06:16.790
One is like 20 and
the other is 24.

00:06:16.790 --> 00:06:18.410
And I'm from the city.

00:06:18.410 --> 00:06:19.580
They don't want to drive.

00:06:19.580 --> 00:06:24.840
And they're waiting for you
to send the self-driving car.

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:27.620
I mean, they're coming, slowly.

00:06:27.620 --> 00:06:29.870
What I like especially
about the self-driving car

00:06:29.870 --> 00:06:33.590
is that it will know about
all other self-driving cars

00:06:33.590 --> 00:06:34.370
in its vicinity.

00:06:34.370 --> 00:06:37.100
So if it had to change
lanes, it just tells them

00:06:37.100 --> 00:06:37.940
I'm changing lanes.

00:06:37.940 --> 00:06:39.860
And then they part the seas.

00:06:39.860 --> 00:06:41.450
The car changes lanes.

00:06:41.450 --> 00:06:44.480
And so the sources
of so many accidents

00:06:44.480 --> 00:06:46.340
would just evaporate overnight.

00:06:46.340 --> 00:06:50.010
And you'd have a safety
factor that is the--

00:06:50.010 --> 00:06:52.100
we're still losing a
hundred people a day

00:06:52.100 --> 00:06:55.670
to car accidents on the
freeways, and highways,

00:06:55.670 --> 00:06:57.920
and pedestrians in
the United States.

00:06:57.920 --> 00:07:02.510
So that would take that to near
zero, if not identically zero.

00:07:02.510 --> 00:07:04.940
In astrophysics, let me think.

00:07:04.940 --> 00:07:06.830
I-- hmm.

00:07:09.986 --> 00:07:12.570
You see, AI--

00:07:12.570 --> 00:07:14.000
I'm-- forgive me.

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:17.360
But I keep thinking
of AI as a tool

00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:20.750
to help me do what I'm
doing rather than what

00:07:20.750 --> 00:07:24.330
I think a full-up AI
should be capable of doing,

00:07:24.330 --> 00:07:27.110
which is completely
replacing me.

00:07:27.110 --> 00:07:30.710
I send the AI to my office,
and let it handle all the phone

00:07:30.710 --> 00:07:32.600
calls, and write the book.

00:07:32.600 --> 00:07:37.430
Because if it's true AI, then
it should be just like me

00:07:37.430 --> 00:07:40.010
or better, especially better.

00:07:40.010 --> 00:07:44.780
And so I don't know where to
put the dart into the AI table

00:07:44.780 --> 00:07:48.290
to say, this is where
I want it versus this

00:07:48.290 --> 00:07:51.230
is where it will ever reach.

00:07:51.230 --> 00:07:54.020
So I don't-- that's probably
a very unsatisfying answer.

00:07:54.020 --> 00:07:58.040
But I still think of AI as
just really good computing,

00:07:58.040 --> 00:08:00.920
helping me solve the
problems that I posed

00:08:00.920 --> 00:08:06.070
and the questions that
I am investigating.

00:08:06.070 --> 00:08:10.090
Well, Neil that's a view
that's consistent with many

00:08:10.090 --> 00:08:10.730
of our viewers.

00:08:10.730 --> 00:08:13.000
We're just trying to make
the tool more capable.

00:08:13.000 --> 00:08:15.070
And someday, I hope
we have a chance--

00:08:15.070 --> 00:08:19.240
I could tell you about how long
till those self-driving cars

00:08:19.240 --> 00:08:21.250
do what you just like.

00:08:21.250 --> 00:08:25.240
But since we're talking
about AI and tools,

00:08:25.240 --> 00:08:27.820
what computing technology
from today would

00:08:27.820 --> 00:08:31.090
you have the most
trouble living without?

00:08:31.090 --> 00:08:33.340
Oh, definitely the smartphone.

00:08:33.340 --> 00:08:38.500
I think the smartphone is the
greatest thing ever conceived

00:08:38.500 --> 00:08:41.530
and invented in the
history of civilization.

00:08:41.530 --> 00:08:44.740
I don't think anything
comes close to it.

00:08:44.740 --> 00:08:47.760
And it happened in our lifetime.

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:49.870
And by the way, any
generation would

00:08:49.870 --> 00:08:52.660
say that about whatever they
might have invented, right.

00:08:52.660 --> 00:08:56.380
Let's go back 140
years or 30 years.

00:08:56.380 --> 00:08:58.660
The telegraph, oh,
my gosh, that's

00:08:58.660 --> 00:09:00.040
going to change the world.

00:09:00.040 --> 00:09:03.550
Oh, we could-- and they lay down
a cable across the Atlantic.

00:09:03.550 --> 00:09:07.810
You talk to Europe, when it
used to take you however long

00:09:07.810 --> 00:09:08.800
to sail.

00:09:08.800 --> 00:09:13.690
And so sure, when you're on
an exponential growth curve,

00:09:13.690 --> 00:09:15.770
if you are anywhere
on that curve,

00:09:15.770 --> 00:09:19.060
It will feel like all the
greatest discoveries happen

00:09:19.060 --> 00:09:22.120
just in the few recent years.

00:09:22.120 --> 00:09:25.600
So it's not that you're
living today in a golden era.

00:09:25.600 --> 00:09:29.350
The entire era is a golden
era because everything

00:09:29.350 --> 00:09:31.130
is happening exponentially.

00:09:31.130 --> 00:09:36.010
And so where do I come off
then saying, the smartphone

00:09:36.010 --> 00:09:38.860
is the greatest thing ever?

00:09:38.860 --> 00:09:44.690
Just simply because of what
technologies it has merged.

00:09:44.690 --> 00:09:48.050
It takes better photos than
most people ever took ever

00:09:48.050 --> 00:09:50.540
in the history of the world.

00:09:50.540 --> 00:09:52.240
More photos are being taken.

00:09:52.240 --> 00:09:56.130
And this started out as a
telephone, a freaking phone.

00:09:56.130 --> 00:09:57.060
OK.

00:09:57.060 --> 00:10:01.520
And I remember the
early camera capacities.

00:10:01.520 --> 00:10:03.150
There was a fuzzy photo.

00:10:03.150 --> 00:10:04.760
And it was wasn't good.

00:10:04.760 --> 00:10:06.750
And it was just like
for an emergency.

00:10:06.750 --> 00:10:11.238
Now, that's my camera of
choice on my telephone.

00:10:11.238 --> 00:10:12.530
Oh, wait, it's not a telephone.

00:10:12.530 --> 00:10:13.940
It's not only a telephone.

00:10:13.940 --> 00:10:17.680
It's access to the whole
world's information.

00:10:17.680 --> 00:10:22.070
Oh, plus, where do I make the
tour to get to grandma's house

00:10:22.070 --> 00:10:23.280
and avoid the traffic?

00:10:23.280 --> 00:10:24.590
Let me check the phone.

00:10:24.590 --> 00:10:29.060
And it's getting information
from satellites, satellites

00:10:29.060 --> 00:10:30.920
that has precision timing.

00:10:30.920 --> 00:10:33.290
And the timing is
so precise it has

00:10:33.290 --> 00:10:38.480
to be corrected for Einstein's
general theory of relativity

00:10:38.480 --> 00:10:42.617
because of the time dilation,
for it being so high up.

00:10:42.617 --> 00:10:44.450
Actually it's the
opposite of time dilation.

00:10:44.450 --> 00:10:49.100
The satellites are so far
away from Earth center,

00:10:49.100 --> 00:10:51.050
and Earth as a
source of gravity,

00:10:51.050 --> 00:10:54.020
that time ticks
differently for it.

00:10:54.020 --> 00:10:56.630
And if it's giving you the
time that you think you need,

00:10:56.630 --> 00:10:59.580
it has to correct for that
before it hands it down.

00:10:59.580 --> 00:11:04.460
So this is a technology that
is using relativity, space

00:11:04.460 --> 00:11:09.470
physics, internet, photography.

00:11:09.470 --> 00:11:14.120
And I'm thinking,
what's left to do?

00:11:14.120 --> 00:11:16.070
So if I were to
go back and bring

00:11:16.070 --> 00:11:19.940
one thing to show Isaac Newton,
it would be the smartphone.

00:11:19.940 --> 00:11:24.410
Well, I'm excited you really
like the technology that was

00:11:24.410 --> 00:11:27.140
produced by our field broadly.

00:11:27.140 --> 00:11:29.430
Do you have any
theories about it?

00:11:29.430 --> 00:11:33.920
And, in particular, maybe
connecting it to AI?

00:11:33.920 --> 00:11:36.320
What is your biggest
fear with respect to AI?

00:11:36.320 --> 00:11:40.100
We know that last year you
agreed with a fellow podcast

00:11:40.100 --> 00:11:43.010
guest, who said that the
world's largest existential

00:11:43.010 --> 00:11:46.580
crisis is within AI.

00:11:46.580 --> 00:11:47.610
What are you afraid of?

00:11:47.610 --> 00:11:49.500
And has your opinion
changed since then?

00:11:49.500 --> 00:11:51.830
I don't think I--
if I agreed, it

00:11:51.830 --> 00:11:54.740
might have only just
been a mild agreement.

00:11:54.740 --> 00:11:58.190
We've all seen the
movies with AI gone bad.

00:11:58.190 --> 00:12:01.100
And often-- I don't
if I said this

00:12:01.100 --> 00:12:03.020
when I was last your guest.

00:12:03.020 --> 00:12:07.910
But the science fiction
writer Ray Bradbury, famously,

00:12:07.910 --> 00:12:13.550
in response to a question posed
to him-- the question was,

00:12:13.550 --> 00:12:18.920
Mr Bradbury why do you write
these future stories of such--

00:12:18.920 --> 00:12:22.710
of science gone bad
and apocalyptic earth.

00:12:22.710 --> 00:12:25.950
Is this the future you
think we're going to have?

00:12:25.950 --> 00:12:28.250
And he, said no, I
write these stories

00:12:28.250 --> 00:12:31.130
so you know what
future to avoid.

00:12:34.350 --> 00:12:36.870
So I don't mind the
apocalyptic movies.

00:12:36.870 --> 00:12:38.310
They sell tickets.

00:12:38.310 --> 00:12:41.100
And they're always
kind of interesting.

00:12:41.100 --> 00:12:48.993
And maybe that'll help reset
our cautionary urges to say

00:12:48.993 --> 00:12:50.160
I don't want that to happen.

00:12:50.160 --> 00:12:51.590
No, that's pretty bad.

00:12:51.590 --> 00:12:53.520
Oh, that-- OK.

00:12:53.520 --> 00:12:56.400
And that's true, of
course, for any technology.

00:12:56.400 --> 00:13:00.690
I think there are other more
tangible existential threats

00:13:00.690 --> 00:13:02.130
than AI.

00:13:02.130 --> 00:13:06.330
And that's why I part ways with
even AI experts, who say this

00:13:06.330 --> 00:13:07.320
and feel this.

00:13:07.320 --> 00:13:13.410
For me, the question is not,
will AI take over the world?

00:13:13.410 --> 00:13:15.300
For me, that's not
the right question.

00:13:15.300 --> 00:13:21.070
The right question is,
when you have AI ability,

00:13:21.070 --> 00:13:25.350
what are you most likely
going to do with it?

00:13:25.350 --> 00:13:29.630
And I don't see anybody--

00:13:29.630 --> 00:13:32.408
maybe in your basement
you doing this.

00:13:32.408 --> 00:13:33.950
And you're not going
to tell anybody.

00:13:33.950 --> 00:13:36.770
But personally, I don't--

00:13:36.770 --> 00:13:37.750
and I've been on--

00:13:37.750 --> 00:13:45.890
I've been-- OK, I'm not like
total computer engineer.

00:13:45.890 --> 00:13:48.710
But I've had my
days on computer,

00:13:48.710 --> 00:13:53.520
I probably coded 50,000 lines
of computer code in my life.

00:13:53.520 --> 00:13:57.050
Most of that in graduate school
and shortly thereafter that,

00:13:57.050 --> 00:13:57.890
and in college.

00:13:57.890 --> 00:14:01.490
About 50,000 lines,
that's respectable.

00:14:01.490 --> 00:14:03.630
But it's not as much as
real professionals at it.

00:14:03.630 --> 00:14:05.750
But I have enough
of a baptism to I

00:14:05.750 --> 00:14:09.410
think-- that my thoughts
have some foundation.

00:14:09.410 --> 00:14:11.630
That's really what I'm
trying to say here.

00:14:11.630 --> 00:14:17.480
So when I think of computers
and what role they play,

00:14:17.480 --> 00:14:20.960
my first thought
is not, let's have

00:14:20.960 --> 00:14:24.810
the computer do everything.

00:14:24.810 --> 00:14:27.150
That's just not my thought.

00:14:27.150 --> 00:14:29.790
My thought is, let me
get a computer that

00:14:29.790 --> 00:14:32.430
makes a perfect cup of coffee.

00:14:32.430 --> 00:14:35.490
Do I need that computer to
guide my self-driving car?

00:14:35.490 --> 00:14:36.240
No.

00:14:36.240 --> 00:14:39.570
Do I need my self-driving
car to run my laptop?

00:14:39.570 --> 00:14:40.350
No.

00:14:40.350 --> 00:14:44.920
Do I need-- so if
you're going to now say

00:14:44.920 --> 00:14:47.130
AI will be all of
this, all wrapped up

00:14:47.130 --> 00:14:51.660
into one humanoid bot,
who's going to do that?

00:14:51.660 --> 00:14:55.140
I can't picture that
as anybody's priority

00:14:55.140 --> 00:14:59.250
because anything you
roll up into one thing,

00:14:59.250 --> 00:15:04.500
I don't think is going to be as
good as the very specifically

00:15:04.500 --> 00:15:09.750
tasked computing
tool that is applied

00:15:09.750 --> 00:15:13.560
to specific things in your
life, making it better,

00:15:13.560 --> 00:15:16.260
and perhaps even perfecting it.

00:15:16.260 --> 00:15:19.020
So I know general
AI is supposed to be

00:15:19.020 --> 00:15:22.560
able to do all of the above.

00:15:22.560 --> 00:15:24.270
Is it going to
walk my dog for me?

00:15:24.270 --> 00:15:25.920
Is it going to--

00:15:25.920 --> 00:15:28.347
is it going to go
to the beach for me?

00:15:28.347 --> 00:15:30.180
People talk about, oh,
we're going to upload

00:15:30.180 --> 00:15:31.680
our brains into computers.

00:15:31.680 --> 00:15:33.690
And then we're in the computer.

00:15:33.690 --> 00:15:35.413
I'm going to take a
stroll on the beach

00:15:35.413 --> 00:15:36.330
and meet three people.

00:15:36.330 --> 00:15:37.590
Did the computer do that?

00:15:37.590 --> 00:15:38.430
No.

00:15:38.430 --> 00:15:39.420
No.

00:15:39.420 --> 00:15:42.540
OK, so it's not me anymore.

00:15:42.540 --> 00:15:46.920
Me, I continue living a life
of a flesh and blood human

00:15:46.920 --> 00:15:49.230
being because I have two
legs and I can walk down

00:15:49.230 --> 00:15:51.060
the frickin' beach.

00:15:51.060 --> 00:15:54.570
Whereas the computer on the
shelf, it can think about it,

00:15:54.570 --> 00:15:56.400
I suppose.

00:15:56.400 --> 00:15:59.302
It can imagine meeting
new people, I suppose.

00:15:59.302 --> 00:16:00.510
And maybe that's good enough.

00:16:00.510 --> 00:16:02.198
And maybe the difference
doesn't matter.

00:16:02.198 --> 00:16:03.990
Like in the movie, The
Matrix, if it's just

00:16:03.990 --> 00:16:07.230
all in the head, who even
knows and who even cares?

00:16:07.230 --> 00:16:09.720
Maybe we're all in
the Matrix right now.

00:16:09.720 --> 00:16:12.662
But I just don't see
the all-purpose computer

00:16:12.662 --> 00:16:13.620
taking over everything.

00:16:13.620 --> 00:16:15.570
I just don't see that.

00:16:15.570 --> 00:16:16.830
I agree.

00:16:16.830 --> 00:16:19.800
Let me move on to
the next question.

00:16:19.800 --> 00:16:21.390
Oh, can I add one thing?

00:16:21.390 --> 00:16:22.080
Sorry.

00:16:22.080 --> 00:16:24.150
Again, I don't remember
if I said this last time.

00:16:24.150 --> 00:16:28.110
I'm old enough to
remember The Jetsons.

00:16:28.110 --> 00:16:32.220
This is a Hanna-Barbera
cartoon, that coexisted in time

00:16:32.220 --> 00:16:33.510
with The Flintstones.

00:16:33.510 --> 00:16:38.190
It's interesting to have the
stone age and the future all as

00:16:38.190 --> 00:16:41.250
prime time cartoons,
half-hour cartoons,

00:16:41.250 --> 00:16:44.550
that were part of the canon
of what I grew up with.

00:16:44.550 --> 00:16:47.310
And in there, of course,
he's in a flying car.

00:16:47.310 --> 00:16:48.780
Of course, it's the future.

00:16:48.780 --> 00:16:51.540
And he's up in the
clouds, of course.

00:16:51.540 --> 00:16:53.080
So what do you have?

00:16:53.080 --> 00:16:58.220
Well, they have a robot maid.

00:16:58.220 --> 00:16:59.420
And it looks like--

00:16:59.420 --> 00:17:04.109
it's wearing like a maid thing.

00:17:04.109 --> 00:17:11.775
And so the maid is pushing the
buttons to clean the laundry

00:17:11.775 --> 00:17:12.650
and to do the things.

00:17:12.650 --> 00:17:16.220
And I'm thinking, no,
you don't need the maid

00:17:16.220 --> 00:17:18.349
to push the button.

00:17:18.349 --> 00:17:22.180
Let the laundry machine
be the computer.

00:17:22.180 --> 00:17:25.700
You don't need a robot to get
in your car, to drive your car.

00:17:25.700 --> 00:17:27.650
Your car is the robot.

00:17:27.650 --> 00:17:30.710
So all of these movies
that show humanoid

00:17:30.710 --> 00:17:32.540
Robots who's going to do that?

00:17:32.540 --> 00:17:33.470
No.

00:17:33.470 --> 00:17:34.820
I've seen some attempts at it.

00:17:34.820 --> 00:17:39.590
I don't see any people lined
up to get a human robot walking

00:17:39.590 --> 00:17:41.210
around their house.

00:17:41.210 --> 00:17:43.280
And if the demand
isn't there, the supply

00:17:43.280 --> 00:17:44.630
is not going to be there.

00:17:44.630 --> 00:17:46.110
So I don't see it.

00:17:46.110 --> 00:17:46.610
Sorry.

00:17:46.610 --> 00:17:49.490
In fact, with that, it's
interesting to think

00:17:49.490 --> 00:17:53.270
about robotic technologies
over the past 50 years.

00:17:53.270 --> 00:17:56.540
Many robots were actually
inspired by the human form,

00:17:56.540 --> 00:17:59.220
with arms and humanoid shapes.

00:17:59.220 --> 00:18:03.590
But we can robotize everything
I need in the built environment.

00:18:03.590 --> 00:18:06.470
We can create so
many more shapes.

00:18:06.470 --> 00:18:10.130
And that's where the
robotics future is.

00:18:10.130 --> 00:18:10.920
Very important.

00:18:10.920 --> 00:18:14.210
And let me echo that because
the idea that you would create

00:18:14.210 --> 00:18:18.230
a robot that's humanoid
comes with the implication

00:18:18.230 --> 00:18:21.110
that the human form
is some ideal thing.

00:18:21.110 --> 00:18:22.100
No.

00:18:22.100 --> 00:18:22.975
No.

00:18:22.975 --> 00:18:25.100
I mean, wouldn't it be
great if you had eight arms,

00:18:25.100 --> 00:18:26.690
like an octopus?

00:18:26.690 --> 00:18:27.980
That would be like really--

00:18:27.980 --> 00:18:29.300
you could be really effective.

00:18:29.300 --> 00:18:32.540
I mean, just be a little
more creative about that.

00:18:32.540 --> 00:18:34.680
To create another human,
I don't need that.

00:18:34.680 --> 00:18:35.450
I love humans.

00:18:35.450 --> 00:18:37.580
I don't need you to
make a robot human.

00:18:37.580 --> 00:18:38.460
No.

00:18:38.460 --> 00:18:38.960
Yeah.

00:18:38.960 --> 00:18:41.980
So you tune the
robot for the task

00:18:41.980 --> 00:18:46.490
physically, kinetically,
so that that task is

00:18:46.490 --> 00:18:49.862
perfected by the morphological
form of that robot.

00:18:49.862 --> 00:18:51.320
And you know
something, when you've

00:18:51.320 --> 00:18:53.840
done that, that robot is not
the robot making your morning

00:18:53.840 --> 00:18:56.330
coffee or your laundry.

00:18:56.330 --> 00:18:58.280
And it's not the one
driving your car.

00:18:58.280 --> 00:19:00.020
And we have a number
of students working

00:19:00.020 --> 00:19:01.880
on exactly those problems.

00:19:01.880 --> 00:19:07.070
But let me move on and ask
you about more astrophysics.

00:19:07.070 --> 00:19:10.400
Harvard's Avi Loeb
argues that Oumuamua

00:19:10.400 --> 00:19:12.740
was an interesting
stellar alien probe.

00:19:12.740 --> 00:19:14.720
What do you think?

00:19:14.720 --> 00:19:17.900
He was able to get
very few people

00:19:17.900 --> 00:19:21.020
to agree with that conclusion
among his colleagues.

00:19:21.020 --> 00:19:25.610
So we can talk about
this outlaying idea.

00:19:25.610 --> 00:19:30.320
And part of what made
it get so much attention

00:19:30.320 --> 00:19:33.350
is his academic
affiliation with Harvard.

00:19:33.350 --> 00:19:36.380
You barely ever saw his
name without Harvard,

00:19:36.380 --> 00:19:37.910
chair of the Harvard department.

00:19:37.910 --> 00:19:40.670
So Harvard was-- he would
ride in that sort of Harvard

00:19:40.670 --> 00:19:44.690
affiliation, in the recognition
he was getting for those ideas.

00:19:44.690 --> 00:19:48.530
Very few people
aligned with that.

00:19:48.530 --> 00:19:52.490
But apart from-- and
by the way, in science,

00:19:52.490 --> 00:19:55.460
if you have a really--
you can have crazy ideas

00:19:55.460 --> 00:19:57.240
that turn out to be true.

00:19:57.240 --> 00:20:00.802
But generally, the effort to
establish that truth is you

00:20:00.802 --> 00:20:01.760
go through peer review.

00:20:01.760 --> 00:20:03.177
And they say, no,
you forgot this.

00:20:03.177 --> 00:20:06.500
And, no, you forgot to cross
the T, dot the I, carry the 2.

00:20:06.500 --> 00:20:09.200
And if you can't get colleagues
to embrace what you just

00:20:09.200 --> 00:20:13.430
came up with, then
maybe you should

00:20:13.430 --> 00:20:15.500
rethink what you're doing.

00:20:15.500 --> 00:20:17.450
That's the whole
point of peer review.

00:20:17.450 --> 00:20:26.710
So anyhow, his paper by the way
that suggested Oumuamua being--

00:20:26.710 --> 00:20:29.870
just to make sure we're
all on the same page, we,

00:20:29.870 --> 00:20:33.130
the astrophysics community,
discovered our very first--

00:20:33.130 --> 00:20:34.880
we've been looking for
these for decades--

00:20:34.880 --> 00:20:38.990
our very first
interstellar visitor

00:20:38.990 --> 00:20:41.060
coming through the solar system.

00:20:41.060 --> 00:20:43.880
And you can determine
that instantaneously

00:20:43.880 --> 00:20:47.120
by establishing its
orbital parameters--

00:20:47.120 --> 00:20:50.120
not instantaneously, you get
a few positions of its orbit.

00:20:50.120 --> 00:20:53.082
And you know, is it
on a hyperbolic orbit,

00:20:53.082 --> 00:20:54.290
is it on an elliptical orbit?

00:20:54.290 --> 00:20:57.600
If it's hyperbolic, it does not
belong to this solar system.

00:20:57.600 --> 00:21:00.200
This we knew from Jump Street.

00:21:00.200 --> 00:21:02.100
It did not belong
in the solar system.

00:21:02.100 --> 00:21:04.500
It got a lot of attention
because of this.

00:21:04.500 --> 00:21:07.490
Then we noticed--
we, the people who--

00:21:07.490 --> 00:21:11.450
the asteroid-- the
solar system folk--

00:21:11.450 --> 00:21:13.730
noticed that it
had what's called

00:21:13.730 --> 00:21:16.130
an anomalous acceleration.

00:21:16.130 --> 00:21:19.112
You add up the gravity of
all the planets, and the Sun,

00:21:19.112 --> 00:21:20.570
and everything else
you know about,

00:21:20.570 --> 00:21:24.080
and it's moving a
little extra than what

00:21:24.080 --> 00:21:27.470
the gravity that should
be influencing it

00:21:27.470 --> 00:21:29.330
could account for.

00:21:29.330 --> 00:21:33.500
And so-- by the way, that's not
so weird in the solar system.

00:21:33.500 --> 00:21:35.130
Comets exhibit
this all the time.

00:21:35.130 --> 00:21:35.630
Why?

00:21:35.630 --> 00:21:41.450
As they near the Sun, heat from
the Sun sublimates the ices.

00:21:41.450 --> 00:21:44.090
And you can get ice jets--

00:21:44.090 --> 00:21:50.410
gaseous jets creating thrust
on what's otherwise a nicely

00:21:50.410 --> 00:21:53.960
Newtonian path,
thereby disrupting

00:21:53.960 --> 00:21:58.460
what you might otherwise think
is the perfect laws of gravity.

00:21:58.460 --> 00:22:01.130
The problem with
Oumuamua is, nobody

00:22:01.130 --> 00:22:02.580
saw any jets coming out of it.

00:22:02.580 --> 00:22:06.510
There was none of this
telltale cometary fuzz.

00:22:06.510 --> 00:22:09.390
And so that meant
it was a conundrum.

00:22:09.390 --> 00:22:12.260
So people for a while said, we
don't know what's causing this.

00:22:12.260 --> 00:22:15.380
Avi Loeb jumped right
in and said "aliens."

00:22:15.380 --> 00:22:18.980
And that's just
not my first guess

00:22:18.980 --> 00:22:21.740
at something if I
don't know what it is.

00:22:21.740 --> 00:22:22.790
I'm going to work--

00:22:22.790 --> 00:22:26.066
I'm going to really--

00:22:26.066 --> 00:22:29.420
I will scratch my head
more before that's

00:22:29.420 --> 00:22:30.830
the conclusion I land on.

00:22:30.830 --> 00:22:33.980
Because if everything
you don't understand you

00:22:33.980 --> 00:22:39.630
want to credit aliens, I
don't know-- what is that?

00:22:39.630 --> 00:22:42.090
And it turns out some
geologists figured it out

00:22:42.090 --> 00:22:44.690
it's probably outgassing
of a kind we're not

00:22:44.690 --> 00:22:47.910
familiar with on solar
system style comets.

00:22:47.910 --> 00:22:50.810
It is after all,
from another place.

00:22:50.810 --> 00:22:54.260
So it easily accounts for it.

00:22:54.260 --> 00:22:56.660
But Avi had his sort
of day in the Sun,

00:22:56.660 --> 00:22:59.570
where everybody wanted to
hear how it is that aliens

00:22:59.570 --> 00:23:01.490
are visiting our solar system.

00:23:01.490 --> 00:23:04.970
By the way, half you
all are physics--

00:23:04.970 --> 00:23:05.840
this MIT, right.

00:23:05.840 --> 00:23:07.760
So you all are physics fluent.

00:23:07.760 --> 00:23:14.990
So anomalous acceleration was
a single extra force operating

00:23:14.990 --> 00:23:20.030
on it, that altered its path
from what would otherwise

00:23:20.030 --> 00:23:23.780
be a pure Newtonian trajectory.

00:23:27.330 --> 00:23:30.680
I'm thinking if
it's aliens, they

00:23:30.680 --> 00:23:33.410
would zigzag through
the solar system.

00:23:33.410 --> 00:23:36.200
They're not going to
still be on a smooth path.

00:23:36.200 --> 00:23:40.130
If they have controlled
acceleration, my gosh,

00:23:40.130 --> 00:23:41.360
nothing is stopping it.

00:23:41.360 --> 00:23:44.750
And if it did a planetary
tour, and hung out, checked out

00:23:44.750 --> 00:23:48.860
the Earth, then I'd say, Avi,
let's revisit that suggestion,

00:23:48.860 --> 00:23:51.800
that maybe aliens
are in control.

00:23:51.800 --> 00:23:53.640
So that's what you have there.

00:23:53.640 --> 00:23:58.310
Oh, by the way, the
monitoring of the sky

00:23:58.310 --> 00:24:01.250
for things that go
bump in the night

00:24:01.250 --> 00:24:06.710
is the sole task of a very new
telescope, which was previously

00:24:06.710 --> 00:24:12.080
known as the Large Synoptic
Survey Telescope, LSST.

00:24:12.080 --> 00:24:16.400
It got renamed thankfully to
the Vera Rubin Telescope, one

00:24:16.400 --> 00:24:19.250
of the more famous
members of our community,

00:24:19.250 --> 00:24:21.950
who discovered dark
matter in galaxies.

00:24:21.950 --> 00:24:24.195
She died a few years
ago, Vera Rubin.

00:24:24.195 --> 00:24:26.040
So it's called the
Vera Rubin Telescope.

00:24:26.040 --> 00:24:28.910
This telescope-- and
it dovetails back

00:24:28.910 --> 00:24:30.650
to your whole existence here.

00:24:30.650 --> 00:24:34.678
This telescope-- forgive me,
I'm not fluent in the numbers.

00:24:34.678 --> 00:24:35.720
I'd have to look them up.

00:24:35.720 --> 00:24:36.980
And they're very lookable.

00:24:36.980 --> 00:24:45.430
But it's basically going to take
a snapshot of the entire sky

00:24:45.430 --> 00:24:49.030
multiple times a
night, thereby making

00:24:49.030 --> 00:24:51.882
a movie of the night sky.

00:24:51.882 --> 00:24:53.590
Now, you say to
yourself, why do you need

00:24:53.590 --> 00:24:55.360
a movie if nothing changes?

00:24:55.360 --> 00:24:57.218
Well, how do you
know nothing changes?

00:24:57.218 --> 00:24:58.510
You've never had a movie of it.

00:25:01.300 --> 00:25:02.800
And there are other
things where you

00:25:02.800 --> 00:25:06.010
can-- if you only observed
something at the same time

00:25:06.010 --> 00:25:11.080
each night, suppose something
blinks on once every 24 hours?

00:25:11.080 --> 00:25:14.350
You'll think the thing is
constantly in the same state

00:25:14.350 --> 00:25:17.170
because you don't have data from
the rest of the phasing of what

00:25:17.170 --> 00:25:18.760
that thing has
happened-- is doing.

00:25:18.760 --> 00:25:20.710
This telescope is
going to solve that.

00:25:20.710 --> 00:25:23.050
And it is the
largest data project

00:25:23.050 --> 00:25:25.120
there ever was in my field.

00:25:25.120 --> 00:25:30.520
And it is almost
entirely computing.

00:25:30.520 --> 00:25:35.050
There's the pipeline of
data that gets analyzed.

00:25:35.050 --> 00:25:37.240
And there's a
serendipity mode, where

00:25:37.240 --> 00:25:39.970
it gets automatically
analyzed and compares it

00:25:39.970 --> 00:25:42.820
with an image the night before
or earlier in that night.

00:25:42.820 --> 00:25:44.260
And if anything is different--

00:25:44.260 --> 00:25:44.760
[SNAP]

00:25:44.760 --> 00:25:47.110
--it goes back to it
and signals other people

00:25:47.110 --> 00:25:50.180
to possibly turn other
telescopes onto that zone.

00:25:50.180 --> 00:25:52.720
So this is modern
astrophysics that

00:25:52.720 --> 00:25:57.070
would be impossible without
very clever programming

00:25:57.070 --> 00:26:00.910
of the management
and analysis of data.

00:26:00.910 --> 00:26:05.090
Where should the Vera Rubin
Telescope look for life?

00:26:05.090 --> 00:26:05.590
[LAUGHTER]

00:26:05.590 --> 00:26:10.820
Well, if it's coming in on a
flying saucer, it'll find it.

00:26:10.820 --> 00:26:12.880
But if you're going
to look for life

00:26:12.880 --> 00:26:14.680
chemically or in
other ways, you want

00:26:14.680 --> 00:26:17.290
to send a probe, maybe
to the planetary surface,

00:26:17.290 --> 00:26:21.610
as we've done with Mars,
the Perseverance rover.

00:26:21.610 --> 00:26:22.450
It's called a rover.

00:26:22.450 --> 00:26:26.480
But it's really a
mobile biolaboratory.

00:26:26.480 --> 00:26:30.980
And it's also got some
geophysics tools in it as well.

00:26:30.980 --> 00:26:33.490
We don't have a word
for Mars geology

00:26:33.490 --> 00:26:36.880
because "geo" means Earth.

00:26:36.880 --> 00:26:40.430
So Mars geology, it's
kind of a misnomer.

00:26:40.430 --> 00:26:43.870
But anyhow, it's doing these
experiments in an attempt

00:26:43.870 --> 00:26:47.620
to establish, and
understand, and characterize

00:26:47.620 --> 00:26:50.410
the environment on Mars,
so that we can pose

00:26:50.410 --> 00:26:51.640
the next round of questions?

00:26:51.640 --> 00:26:52.960
Was there ever life there?

00:26:52.960 --> 00:26:54.880
Is there life there now?

00:26:54.880 --> 00:26:57.160
If it's there now,
is it underground,

00:26:57.160 --> 00:26:58.540
and how far underground?

00:26:58.540 --> 00:27:01.030
And if there's water
there, is the water liquid,

00:27:01.030 --> 00:27:03.790
is there a heat source that
we haven't reckoned before?

00:27:03.790 --> 00:27:06.700
All of these questions get
asked in the search for life.

00:27:06.700 --> 00:27:08.890
But I can tell you that
if a flying saucer comes

00:27:08.890 --> 00:27:11.590
through our airspace,
the Vera Rubin Telescope

00:27:11.590 --> 00:27:14.920
is going to catch it early.

00:27:14.920 --> 00:27:18.160
Speaking of looking
for life on Mars,

00:27:18.160 --> 00:27:21.070
do you expect to see
life in the seas of Titan

00:27:21.070 --> 00:27:23.815
in our solar system?

00:27:23.815 --> 00:27:25.640
So Titan-- so let's--

00:27:25.640 --> 00:27:27.860
so just again, to
catch people up.

00:27:27.860 --> 00:27:30.810
Titan is one of the
moons of Saturn.

00:27:30.810 --> 00:27:33.830
And, in fact, it's
one of the biggest

00:27:33.830 --> 00:27:36.890
moons in the whole
solar system actually.

00:27:36.890 --> 00:27:40.240
And it has an atmosphere.

00:27:40.240 --> 00:27:43.420
Oh, my God, a moon
with an atmosphere.

00:27:43.420 --> 00:27:45.170
Well, if you have an
atmosphere that means

00:27:45.170 --> 00:27:47.840
you can establish
partial pressures

00:27:47.840 --> 00:27:51.560
on things that might then
be sustained as liquid.

00:27:51.560 --> 00:27:54.320
Once you lose your atmosphere,
liquids are not stable.

00:27:54.320 --> 00:27:58.040
And they just evaporate out.

00:27:58.040 --> 00:28:01.970
So if Titan has an atmosphere,
let's look for liquids.

00:28:01.970 --> 00:28:03.740
And what's the
atmosphere made of?

00:28:03.740 --> 00:28:06.950
Well, it's mostly methane, CH3.

00:28:06.950 --> 00:28:08.450
So you got methane.

00:28:08.450 --> 00:28:10.880
But Titan is way out there.

00:28:10.880 --> 00:28:12.110
Jupiter's far enough.

00:28:12.110 --> 00:28:13.580
Now, you double that.

00:28:13.580 --> 00:28:15.073
And you get to Saturn.

00:28:15.073 --> 00:28:16.490
And the moon around
Saturn, that's

00:28:16.490 --> 00:28:20.900
way outside the Goldilocks zone.

00:28:20.900 --> 00:28:23.090
So we're not going to
expect liquid water.

00:28:23.090 --> 00:28:24.680
No, I don't think.

00:28:24.680 --> 00:28:25.910
So you get there.

00:28:25.910 --> 00:28:27.338
And there are these boulders.

00:28:27.338 --> 00:28:28.880
Because we've landed
on the surface--

00:28:28.880 --> 00:28:34.280
a European probe, attached to an
American orbiter around Saturn,

00:28:34.280 --> 00:28:36.980
Huygens Probe, named
after Christian Huygens,

00:28:36.980 --> 00:28:42.740
a polymath, a Dutch polymath,
from the era, from the 1600s--

00:28:42.740 --> 00:28:43.820
and 1700s.

00:28:43.820 --> 00:28:47.750
So he-- the Huygens
Probe deployed

00:28:47.750 --> 00:28:50.300
and descended onto
the surface of Titan,

00:28:50.300 --> 00:28:51.440
through the atmosphere.

00:28:51.440 --> 00:28:53.690
And it's able to make a soft
landing-- because there's

00:28:53.690 --> 00:28:56.610
an atmosphere, you can
just use a parachute.

00:28:56.610 --> 00:28:59.410
When we landed on the Moon,
you couldn't use a parachute.

00:28:59.410 --> 00:29:03.240
You needed to spend fuel to
prevent yourself from crashing.

00:29:03.240 --> 00:29:05.070
This is part of the
fuel budget that you

00:29:05.070 --> 00:29:07.020
have to consider
depending on where you

00:29:07.020 --> 00:29:08.770
want to go in the solar system.

00:29:08.770 --> 00:29:09.727
So here's Titan.

00:29:09.727 --> 00:29:12.060
But then you ask, well, what's
the temperature of Titan?

00:29:12.060 --> 00:29:13.350
And I forgot what it is.

00:29:13.350 --> 00:29:17.760
But it's cold enough
to liquefy methane.

00:29:17.760 --> 00:29:19.407
Oh, my gosh.

00:29:19.407 --> 00:29:21.990
Well, if we can liquefy methane,
and there's partial pressures

00:29:21.990 --> 00:29:25.670
to keep it liquid, we
should look for lakes.

00:29:25.670 --> 00:29:31.700
And, oh, my gosh, we found
lakes, methane lakes on Titan.

00:29:31.700 --> 00:29:33.860
They were closer to the
poles than we had thought.

00:29:33.860 --> 00:29:36.090
Maybe they'd be more
towards the equator.

00:29:36.090 --> 00:29:39.380
So-- all right, well,
where's the water

00:29:39.380 --> 00:29:42.530
because really water is not
uncommon in the universe?

00:29:42.530 --> 00:29:46.340
Oh, the water is
frozen into bedrock.

00:29:46.340 --> 00:29:50.840
So here on Earth when we see
rocks, we say rocks are solid.

00:29:50.840 --> 00:29:53.840
No, rocks are solid
at room temperature.

00:29:53.840 --> 00:29:57.080
Go inside a volcano,
rocks are liquid.

00:29:57.080 --> 00:29:59.210
So what is water on Titan?

00:29:59.210 --> 00:30:01.070
It is frozen solid.

00:30:01.070 --> 00:30:02.660
It is the bedrock.

00:30:02.660 --> 00:30:04.280
And those are the mountains.

00:30:04.280 --> 00:30:05.930
Those are water.

00:30:05.930 --> 00:30:08.430
And the liquid is
liquid methane.

00:30:08.430 --> 00:30:13.780
So let's ask the question,
what does it take to have life?

00:30:13.780 --> 00:30:15.520
Are we going to
invoke a water bias

00:30:15.520 --> 00:30:17.910
and say, life as we know it.

00:30:17.910 --> 00:30:19.160
It's got to have liquid water.

00:30:19.160 --> 00:30:20.452
So let's look for liquid water?

00:30:20.452 --> 00:30:26.030
Maybe all life really
needs is liquid.

00:30:26.030 --> 00:30:29.660
And maybe methane--
liquid methane--

00:30:29.660 --> 00:30:32.690
is a whole-- is a foundation
for a whole other kind

00:30:32.690 --> 00:30:36.740
of life form, that our
imagination prevents us

00:30:36.740 --> 00:30:43.380
from conjuring because we are
so earth-biased in our biology.

00:30:43.380 --> 00:30:47.030
So I like the question,
could there be life on Titan?

00:30:47.030 --> 00:30:50.390
Because if there is,
it will completely

00:30:50.390 --> 00:30:56.510
stretch our understanding of
all the ways of being alive.

00:30:56.510 --> 00:31:01.410
What chemistry do expect
non-DNA form to use?

00:31:01.410 --> 00:31:04.280
So here's an interesting--
let me sort of not answer

00:31:04.280 --> 00:31:07.610
that question, with
another comment.

00:31:07.610 --> 00:31:11.270
If you speak to geologists,
and they look at Mars,

00:31:11.270 --> 00:31:12.560
they see rocks.

00:31:12.560 --> 00:31:15.063
And they say, oh,
there are iron oxides.

00:31:15.063 --> 00:31:15.980
And there's silicates.

00:31:15.980 --> 00:31:19.730
And they can use
the same vocabulary

00:31:19.730 --> 00:31:21.680
that they use on Earth
when they're talking

00:31:21.680 --> 00:31:24.420
about the rocks on Mars.

00:31:24.420 --> 00:31:28.530
So what it means is minerals--

00:31:28.530 --> 00:31:32.310
geology as a general field--

00:31:32.310 --> 00:31:36.120
is highly portable
in your understanding

00:31:36.120 --> 00:31:42.090
of processes from one
rocky planet to another.

00:31:42.090 --> 00:31:45.380
Well, if that's-- let's call
that a universal thing that

00:31:45.380 --> 00:31:49.910
minerals do under certain
temperatures and pressures.

00:31:49.910 --> 00:31:55.640
Should we not presume that
the way life formed on Earth

00:31:55.640 --> 00:32:00.740
wouldn't be exactly the way
life would form anywhere else?

00:32:00.740 --> 00:32:05.390
Should we require that
identity gets encoded in ways

00:32:05.390 --> 00:32:07.970
that do not invoke DNA?

00:32:07.970 --> 00:32:11.552
When geologists are not
saying, oh, here's an element,

00:32:11.552 --> 00:32:12.260
we've never-- no.

00:32:12.260 --> 00:32:13.430
We've got the elements.

00:32:13.430 --> 00:32:15.230
We've got the periodic table.

00:32:15.230 --> 00:32:17.072
Planets are not
inventing new elements

00:32:17.072 --> 00:32:18.530
that we haven't
already discovered.

00:32:18.530 --> 00:32:20.540
We've got that, OK.

00:32:20.540 --> 00:32:24.470
So could it be that
biological processes

00:32:24.470 --> 00:32:28.850
are as repeatable as
the formation of rocks?

00:32:28.850 --> 00:32:30.680
That's an interesting question.

00:32:30.680 --> 00:32:34.490
And if that's the
case, then we could

00:32:34.490 --> 00:32:38.510
declare that the DNA molecule
is an inevitable consequence

00:32:38.510 --> 00:32:40.750
of complex chemistry.

00:32:40.750 --> 00:32:43.510
And no longer have to
ask ourselves, what

00:32:43.510 --> 00:32:46.390
would life be like without DNA?

00:32:46.390 --> 00:32:48.580
So that's a way to not
answer your question,

00:32:48.580 --> 00:32:53.050
but with another sort of
probing thought about this.

00:32:53.050 --> 00:32:57.940
And by the way, life on
Earth formed really fast.

00:32:57.940 --> 00:33:00.550
We've been around 4
and 1/2 billion years.

00:33:00.550 --> 00:33:04.480
The first half a billion, the
conditions on Earth's surface

00:33:04.480 --> 00:33:05.830
were very hot.

00:33:05.830 --> 00:33:09.020
We were still accreting
material from the solar system.

00:33:09.020 --> 00:33:12.615
So that would break
apart complex molecules.

00:33:12.615 --> 00:33:16.660
The binding energy is less than
the impact energy of rocks.

00:33:16.660 --> 00:33:19.060
So then, finally,
we cooled down.

00:33:19.060 --> 00:33:23.230
Now, we can make
complex chemistry.

00:33:23.230 --> 00:33:24.970
Start the clock
at that point, it

00:33:24.970 --> 00:33:28.840
took a hundred million
years for life to show up.

00:33:28.840 --> 00:33:30.830
Yeah, that sounds
like a long time.

00:33:30.830 --> 00:33:34.730
But what's a hundred million
years compared with 4 billion?

00:33:34.730 --> 00:33:39.530
So left to itself,
Earth made life.

00:33:39.530 --> 00:33:41.720
And so why should I think--

00:33:41.720 --> 00:33:44.270
and just because we have a hard
time making life in the lab--

00:33:44.270 --> 00:33:45.520
we haven't figured it out yet.

00:33:45.520 --> 00:33:47.150
We got the top
people on it though.

00:33:47.150 --> 00:33:48.200
Just as we haven't
figured it out,

00:33:48.200 --> 00:33:49.867
doesn't mean nature
didn't figure it out

00:33:49.867 --> 00:33:52.640
or that nature won't figure
it out wherever it has

00:33:52.640 --> 00:33:54.290
access to complex chemistry.

00:33:54.290 --> 00:33:56.660
And as your audience
surely knows,

00:33:56.660 --> 00:33:59.960
all the ingredients of life,
the hydrogen, the oxygen,

00:33:59.960 --> 00:34:02.000
the silicon, the
carbon, all of that

00:34:02.000 --> 00:34:06.450
are the most common ingredients
across the universe.

00:34:06.450 --> 00:34:11.900
So if we were made of
a isotope of bismuth,

00:34:11.900 --> 00:34:15.050
you could say, yeah, life
is rare, life as we know it.

00:34:15.050 --> 00:34:16.280
We're special.

00:34:16.280 --> 00:34:21.560
But, no, no, the
top three chemically

00:34:21.560 --> 00:34:24.590
active ingredients in
the universe in order

00:34:24.590 --> 00:34:27.780
are the order in which you
find them in life on Earth.

00:34:27.780 --> 00:34:28.280
Excuse me.

00:34:28.280 --> 00:34:29.060
It's the top four.

00:34:29.060 --> 00:34:32.929
So hydrogen is the number
one element in life.

00:34:32.929 --> 00:34:35.060
The number two element in
the universe is helium.

00:34:35.060 --> 00:34:36.260
But it's chemically inert.

00:34:36.260 --> 00:34:37.159
It doesn't count.

00:34:37.159 --> 00:34:38.060
What's next?

00:34:38.060 --> 00:34:41.659
Carbon, oxygen, these
next two elements

00:34:41.659 --> 00:34:44.480
are two and three
in the universe.

00:34:44.480 --> 00:34:47.090
And so oxygen is more
abundant than carbon.

00:34:47.090 --> 00:34:48.989
And that's what we're made of.

00:34:48.989 --> 00:34:53.056
So life was really opportunistic
with its ingredients.

00:34:53.056 --> 00:34:54.889
And plus, we got carbon
in the middle of it,

00:34:54.889 --> 00:34:58.790
which is extraordinarily
sticky, chemically sticky.

00:34:58.790 --> 00:35:02.120
Man, it can bind to itself,
with its four electrons.

00:35:02.120 --> 00:35:03.830
And it can bind
with another four.

00:35:03.830 --> 00:35:05.240
It'll get two, two, and two.

00:35:05.240 --> 00:35:08.480
It can get one, one, one, and
one-- so one, one, and one.

00:35:08.480 --> 00:35:10.700
And it can make
long chains, just

00:35:10.700 --> 00:35:13.850
like what the double helix is.

00:35:13.850 --> 00:35:17.990
So anyhow, I'm going to vote
for the double helix, at least

00:35:17.990 --> 00:35:20.010
for now.

00:35:20.010 --> 00:35:21.540
That's great.

00:35:21.540 --> 00:35:24.600
OK, more about our universe.

00:35:24.600 --> 00:35:28.146
Is our universe expanding
within a different universe?

00:35:28.146 --> 00:35:28.960
I'm sorry.

00:35:28.960 --> 00:35:30.115
Are we expanding--

00:35:30.115 --> 00:35:34.210
Is our universe expanding
within a different universe?

00:35:34.210 --> 00:35:34.730
Oh, yes.

00:35:34.730 --> 00:35:37.970
So let me shape
that up a bit here.

00:35:37.970 --> 00:35:43.570
So we have modern understanding
of the application

00:35:43.570 --> 00:35:47.110
of quantum physics to the
early universe, the Big Bang

00:35:47.110 --> 00:35:49.490
basically.

00:35:49.490 --> 00:35:53.130
It allows us-- if you only use
Einsteinian physics for the Big

00:35:53.130 --> 00:35:55.520
Bang, it's kind of limiting.

00:35:55.520 --> 00:35:57.740
But when the whole
universe-- quantum physics

00:35:57.740 --> 00:35:59.270
is the physics of the small.

00:35:59.270 --> 00:36:02.060
And general relativity is
the physics of the large.

00:36:02.060 --> 00:36:05.150
But at the Big Bang,
the large was small.

00:36:05.150 --> 00:36:09.080
So if the large is small and
rivals the sizes of particles,

00:36:09.080 --> 00:36:13.160
then you get to apply
quantum rules that

00:36:13.160 --> 00:36:15.710
impact the entire universe.

00:36:15.710 --> 00:36:17.660
That's a fun place to hang out.

00:36:17.660 --> 00:36:19.640
It's also a shotgun
wedding there

00:36:19.640 --> 00:36:24.770
because the two don't really
play well in the sandbox.

00:36:24.770 --> 00:36:27.802
You need a third understanding
of how this works.

00:36:27.802 --> 00:36:29.510
And this is what string
theorists come in

00:36:29.510 --> 00:36:31.850
and other high-level
cosmologists.

00:36:31.850 --> 00:36:37.730
But when you follow some natural
consequences of this marriage,

00:36:37.730 --> 00:36:42.710
one of the thing-- and with
an inflationary scenario--

00:36:42.710 --> 00:36:47.390
inflation, Alan Guth, an MIT
professor, one of the pioneers

00:36:47.390 --> 00:36:48.830
of that concept--

00:36:48.830 --> 00:36:55.430
when you invoke this,
you learn that this thing

00:36:55.430 --> 00:36:59.990
can make multiple universes,
multiple expanding bubbles.

00:36:59.990 --> 00:37:05.490
And so if that's
the case, then we

00:37:05.490 --> 00:37:12.390
are one expanding bubble inside
of a larger universe, that

00:37:12.390 --> 00:37:15.390
has other expanding
bubbles within it.

00:37:15.390 --> 00:37:17.630
Now, you could be
an expanding bubble

00:37:17.630 --> 00:37:20.810
in an expanding universe,
where everybody is expanding.

00:37:20.810 --> 00:37:24.020
And you'll never actually
bump into one another.

00:37:24.020 --> 00:37:26.630
If the universe is expanding
faster than your bubble is,

00:37:26.630 --> 00:37:28.160
for example.

00:37:28.160 --> 00:37:29.990
So then you'll never catch up.

00:37:29.990 --> 00:37:32.840
And that's an
interesting scenario.

00:37:32.840 --> 00:37:37.130
You can rank these multiverses
by their simplicity

00:37:37.130 --> 00:37:38.810
or their complexity.

00:37:38.810 --> 00:37:41.870
And that one would be the
simplest example of one.

00:37:41.870 --> 00:37:44.300
Because these other
universes still share--

00:37:44.300 --> 00:37:46.310
in a way, they share
our space-time.

00:37:46.310 --> 00:37:48.433
They'd have the same
laws of physics.

00:37:48.433 --> 00:37:49.850
They might have a
different amount

00:37:49.850 --> 00:37:53.030
of matter in them or whatever.

00:37:53.030 --> 00:37:55.370
And there could be an
infinite number of them.

00:37:55.370 --> 00:37:57.967
In this book, that just
came out a month ago--

00:37:57.967 --> 00:37:58.550
can I show it?

00:37:58.550 --> 00:37:59.105
Is that--

00:37:59.105 --> 00:37:59.605
Go ahead.

00:37:59.605 --> 00:38:01.580
Cosmic Queries.

00:38:01.580 --> 00:38:03.530
I've got to show it there--

00:38:03.530 --> 00:38:06.110
Cosmic Queries.

00:38:06.110 --> 00:38:09.410
This is a book that's collected
the deepest questions we've

00:38:09.410 --> 00:38:13.640
ever asked about the
universe in the history

00:38:13.640 --> 00:38:14.750
of our civilization.

00:38:14.750 --> 00:38:20.270
So in here is how did
it all begin, how did it

00:38:20.270 --> 00:38:23.720
get to be this way,
what is it all made of,

00:38:23.720 --> 00:38:26.090
how will it all end?

00:38:26.090 --> 00:38:28.310
And there's a whole
section here on,

00:38:28.310 --> 00:38:31.033
what does nothing have
to do with everything?

00:38:31.033 --> 00:38:32.450
Because how do you
define nothing?

00:38:32.450 --> 00:38:33.802
Is it where there's no matter?

00:38:33.802 --> 00:38:35.010
Well, suppose there's energy?

00:38:35.010 --> 00:38:36.390
OK, let's take away the energy.

00:38:36.390 --> 00:38:37.640
Is there no quantum phenomena?

00:38:37.640 --> 00:38:38.990
Let's take that away.

00:38:38.990 --> 00:38:41.150
Well, there's still the
fabric of space and time

00:38:41.150 --> 00:38:42.920
getting curved by gravity.

00:38:42.920 --> 00:38:43.857
Let's take that away.

00:38:43.857 --> 00:38:46.190
Well, then there's still the
laws of physics that apply.

00:38:46.190 --> 00:38:47.695
Let's take that away.

00:38:47.695 --> 00:38:49.820
So what does it mean then
to have a region of space

00:38:49.820 --> 00:38:52.028
where you don't even have
laws of physics that apply?

00:38:52.028 --> 00:38:53.990
We go there in this
book because there's

00:38:53.990 --> 00:38:59.000
a category of questions that
are just dangling by our ankles

00:38:59.000 --> 00:39:00.770
off the cliff.

00:39:00.770 --> 00:39:03.500
And we're dangling
there because we don't--

00:39:03.500 --> 00:39:06.350
let's just-- is the question
even the right question

00:39:06.350 --> 00:39:09.410
that were asking?

00:39:09.410 --> 00:39:11.060
So that's one
category of universe.

00:39:11.060 --> 00:39:13.430
A whole other
category of universe

00:39:13.430 --> 00:39:16.880
allows the different multiverses
to have different laws

00:39:16.880 --> 00:39:19.790
of physics within them.

00:39:19.790 --> 00:39:23.430
and they get more and
more spooky and scary,

00:39:23.430 --> 00:39:26.300
actually for me, because I
lose sleep over some of these.

00:39:26.300 --> 00:39:29.660
And so it's a fun
exercise in pushing

00:39:29.660 --> 00:39:31.550
the limits of your
physics and your math

00:39:31.550 --> 00:39:33.980
to see what fate awaits us.

00:39:33.980 --> 00:39:36.260
And for me, the scariest
fate is the possibility

00:39:36.260 --> 00:39:39.510
that the accelerated
expansion of the universe--

00:39:39.510 --> 00:39:43.370
this is that dark energy
you may have read about.

00:39:43.370 --> 00:39:47.030
If that goes
unchecked, then that

00:39:47.030 --> 00:39:51.350
stretching of the
fabric of space and time

00:39:51.350 --> 00:39:59.580
will overcome the elasticity
of space-time itself.

00:39:59.580 --> 00:40:03.510
And when that
happens, it will rip

00:40:03.510 --> 00:40:07.830
at the pixelated level of
what the universe-- of what

00:40:07.830 --> 00:40:09.190
constructs the universe.

00:40:09.190 --> 00:40:10.800
And that's the Planck length.

00:40:10.800 --> 00:40:13.980
This Planck length,
all right, that

00:40:13.980 --> 00:40:15.630
is the quantization
of the universe

00:40:15.630 --> 00:40:17.500
that general
relativity doesn't see.

00:40:17.500 --> 00:40:20.500
That's why you needed a third
theory to put them together.

00:40:20.500 --> 00:40:23.310
But that pixelization of
the universe, as you expand,

00:40:23.310 --> 00:40:25.860
there's a point where
it wants to expand that.

00:40:25.860 --> 00:40:31.050
How do you expand the basic
building pixel of the universe?

00:40:31.050 --> 00:40:31.710
You can't.

00:40:31.710 --> 00:40:33.075
So it's called the Big Rip.

00:40:33.075 --> 00:40:34.500
And if it goes
unchecked, that'll

00:40:34.500 --> 00:40:37.170
happen in about
20 billion years.

00:40:37.170 --> 00:40:38.790
So that's what I
really lose sleep on,

00:40:38.790 --> 00:40:41.190
if you want to talk
about other universes.

00:40:41.190 --> 00:40:44.370
Time to move to
another universe then.

00:40:44.370 --> 00:40:48.360
Speaking of our own universe,
how will our galaxy still

00:40:48.360 --> 00:40:52.030
collide with Andromeda
despite this expansion?

00:40:52.030 --> 00:40:52.530
Good.

00:40:52.530 --> 00:40:55.030
It'll collide before
that becomes a problem.

00:40:55.030 --> 00:40:56.715
[LAUGHTER]

00:40:58.740 --> 00:41:03.930
So as we continue to expand,
the strength of the dark energy

00:41:03.930 --> 00:41:07.500
gains relative to the
strength of gravity.

00:41:07.500 --> 00:41:12.540
And right now, we can still have
solar systems, and galaxies,

00:41:12.540 --> 00:41:15.890
and nearby galaxies falling
towards one another.

00:41:15.890 --> 00:41:18.720
You go a little too far away,
the expansion of the universe

00:41:18.720 --> 00:41:23.080
is overriding the urge of the
galaxies to come together.

00:41:23.080 --> 00:41:25.230
And that's where we get
our whole understanding

00:41:25.230 --> 00:41:26.670
of the expanding universe.

00:41:26.670 --> 00:41:29.520
But nearby galaxies,
our strength of gravity

00:41:29.520 --> 00:41:31.920
strong enough so that the
expanding universe is not

00:41:31.920 --> 00:41:34.030
overtaking this just yet.

00:41:34.030 --> 00:41:38.160
And we will collide with
Andromeda in 6, 7 billion years

00:41:38.160 --> 00:41:38.850
from now.

00:41:38.850 --> 00:41:40.710
And we even have a word
for that new galaxy.

00:41:40.710 --> 00:41:42.390
I thought it was very creative.

00:41:42.390 --> 00:41:47.700
It's called the
Milkomeda or Milkomedia.

00:41:47.700 --> 00:41:50.130
There's Andromeda
and the Milky Way--

00:41:50.130 --> 00:41:52.045
Milkomedia.

00:41:52.045 --> 00:41:52.920
It's a little clumsy.

00:41:52.920 --> 00:41:54.590
But it's cute.

00:41:54.590 --> 00:41:57.560
How close are we to finding
a unified equation that

00:41:57.560 --> 00:41:59.240
explains all matter?

00:41:59.240 --> 00:42:01.400
So I have a lot of
thoughts about that,

00:42:01.400 --> 00:42:04.100
not that I have
any actual talent

00:42:04.100 --> 00:42:06.590
to participate in that exercise.

00:42:06.590 --> 00:42:08.108
but I think a lot about it.

00:42:08.108 --> 00:42:08.900
And I just wonder--

00:42:11.840 --> 00:42:16.820
that very question
contains a very deep bias,

00:42:16.820 --> 00:42:18.980
that the universe
actually can be

00:42:18.980 --> 00:42:21.810
described by a simple equation.

00:42:21.810 --> 00:42:24.450
That's an interesting
philosophical bias.

00:42:24.450 --> 00:42:29.130
You're imparting your own
sense of order and elegance

00:42:29.130 --> 00:42:31.800
onto a very complex universe.

00:42:31.800 --> 00:42:33.450
And I try to stay open minded.

00:42:33.450 --> 00:42:36.610
Maybe the universe
is just messy.

00:42:36.610 --> 00:42:40.560
Maybe the all forces don't
come together as one.

00:42:40.560 --> 00:42:41.820
Maybe.

00:42:41.820 --> 00:42:43.190
I'm going to allow for that.

00:42:43.190 --> 00:42:44.050
You know why?

00:42:44.050 --> 00:42:46.140
Because I studied
Johannes Kepler,

00:42:46.140 --> 00:42:48.750
mathematician, German
mathematician, around the time

00:42:48.750 --> 00:42:52.220
of Galileo, and Shakespeare,
and Queen Elizabeth.

00:42:52.220 --> 00:42:56.460
A very fertile time in
Europe, around the year 1600.

00:42:56.460 --> 00:42:59.020
He was sure-- because
he was a mathematician.

00:42:59.020 --> 00:43:00.990
And he knew about
the Platonic solids.

00:43:00.990 --> 00:43:03.870
You know about this if
you know any geometry.

00:43:03.870 --> 00:43:06.100
There's only five
Platonic solids.

00:43:06.100 --> 00:43:09.360
So these are solid shapes,
where every surface

00:43:09.360 --> 00:43:14.350
is an identical polygon, an
identical, uniform polygon--

00:43:14.350 --> 00:43:15.670
so a triangle.

00:43:15.670 --> 00:43:16.650
So what do you have?

00:43:16.650 --> 00:43:19.260
You can have-- four triangles
will make a pyramid.

00:43:19.260 --> 00:43:22.590
That's one of the
five Platonic solids.

00:43:22.590 --> 00:43:24.030
Another one is a cube.

00:43:24.030 --> 00:43:25.132
Each side is a square.

00:43:25.132 --> 00:43:27.090
If you keep doing this,
there are five of them,

00:43:27.090 --> 00:43:30.660
it turns-- and no
more, and no fewer.

00:43:30.660 --> 00:43:31.652
He loved math.

00:43:31.652 --> 00:43:33.360
And he knew the universe
must be perfect.

00:43:33.360 --> 00:43:34.050
God made it.

00:43:34.050 --> 00:43:34.710
God's perfect.

00:43:34.710 --> 00:43:35.640
Math is perfect.

00:43:35.640 --> 00:43:40.320
So clearly the movement of
the planets around the Sun

00:43:40.320 --> 00:43:44.170
must have-- and there's six
known planets, Mercury Venus,

00:43:44.170 --> 00:43:45.960
Earth, Mars,
Jupiter, and Saturn.

00:43:45.960 --> 00:43:48.300
And he has five of these solids.

00:43:48.300 --> 00:43:52.350
So he decided to nest
the solids and then

00:43:52.350 --> 00:43:57.000
put orbits in the
gaps between them

00:43:57.000 --> 00:43:59.190
because if you have five
solids, you have six gaps,

00:43:59.190 --> 00:44:01.425
or there's an
outer zone as well.

00:44:01.425 --> 00:44:03.300
And if you do that, he's
saying, yeah, that's

00:44:03.300 --> 00:44:05.910
got to be it because
that is beautiful,

00:44:05.910 --> 00:44:09.120
because it is simple, because
that's how it must be.

00:44:09.120 --> 00:44:13.410
And he spent 10 years
working on this.

00:44:13.410 --> 00:44:16.457
And he said, no, it's not that.

00:44:16.457 --> 00:44:17.790
And they're not perfect circles.

00:44:17.790 --> 00:44:19.170
No, they're ellipses.

00:44:19.170 --> 00:44:22.320
And they're different
shape ellipses at that.

00:44:22.320 --> 00:44:27.480
And so the whole,
perfect philosophy

00:44:27.480 --> 00:44:29.940
of what was guiding
his inquiry had

00:44:29.940 --> 00:44:32.020
to be thrown out the window.

00:44:32.020 --> 00:44:36.030
So maybe there's a
unified field theory

00:44:36.030 --> 00:44:38.190
that captures all
the forces of nature,

00:44:38.190 --> 00:44:40.620
simplifying everything in the
universe into one equation,

00:44:40.620 --> 00:44:42.250
maybe.

00:44:42.250 --> 00:44:44.800
But my read of history
tells me every time

00:44:44.800 --> 00:44:49.270
we layer our own requirements
on what nature ought to be,

00:44:49.270 --> 00:44:51.530
we end up with egg in our face.

00:44:51.530 --> 00:44:53.170
So I'm not--

00:44:53.170 --> 00:44:55.120
I'm not betting on it.

00:44:55.120 --> 00:44:59.190
Should we put more effort
into colonizing Mars?

00:44:59.190 --> 00:45:02.740
I would ask why would you
want to colonize Mars?

00:45:02.740 --> 00:45:04.238
What's your goal?

00:45:04.238 --> 00:45:05.280
I'm asking you right now?

00:45:08.420 --> 00:45:11.720
Well, the idea of
decolonizing Mars

00:45:11.720 --> 00:45:16.550
could help us from escaping
our climate Armageddon,

00:45:16.550 --> 00:45:17.270
for instance.

00:45:17.270 --> 00:45:18.260
OK, good.

00:45:18.260 --> 00:45:18.810
Good.

00:45:18.810 --> 00:45:20.310
So that's a common thought.

00:45:20.310 --> 00:45:21.560
It makes a good headline, too.

00:45:21.560 --> 00:45:29.570
And Elon Musk, and Carl
Sagan, and Stephen Hawking,

00:45:29.570 --> 00:45:33.440
some very visible thinkers
in recent decades,

00:45:33.440 --> 00:45:35.180
have made very
strong suggestions

00:45:35.180 --> 00:45:37.910
that we should become at
least a two-planet species,

00:45:37.910 --> 00:45:40.280
to ensure our survival
in case there's

00:45:40.280 --> 00:45:43.730
some kind of catastrophe
or Armageddon rendering us

00:45:43.730 --> 00:45:45.555
extinct on one of the planets.

00:45:45.555 --> 00:45:46.680
That makes a good headline.

00:45:46.680 --> 00:45:50.090
But I'm a little more
practical than that.

00:45:50.090 --> 00:45:53.843
So I'm swimming
upstream in the ideas

00:45:53.843 --> 00:45:55.010
I'm about to share with you.

00:45:55.010 --> 00:45:56.390
They're not ideas.

00:45:56.390 --> 00:45:59.860
They're just-- I'm just--

00:45:59.860 --> 00:46:02.390
it's my sensibility is
different from that of others.

00:46:02.390 --> 00:46:04.305
And here's why.

00:46:04.305 --> 00:46:06.520
If you want to
colonize Mars now,

00:46:06.520 --> 00:46:09.640
well, you can't because
you can't breathe the air.

00:46:09.640 --> 00:46:12.640
So you have to build some
kind of habitat module.

00:46:12.640 --> 00:46:15.070
So if you build
a habitat module,

00:46:15.070 --> 00:46:16.690
and then you go to
Mars, well, you're

00:46:16.690 --> 00:46:17.960
not really living on Mars.

00:46:17.960 --> 00:46:20.590
You're living on Earth,
on Mars, because you just

00:46:20.590 --> 00:46:22.090
created a habitat.

00:46:22.090 --> 00:46:24.050
Well, that's like
going to Disneyland.

00:46:24.050 --> 00:46:26.350
I don't mind visiting Mars,
and going to Disneyland,

00:46:26.350 --> 00:46:29.720
spending a few weeks,
and then coming back.

00:46:29.720 --> 00:46:30.407
Sure.

00:46:30.407 --> 00:46:31.990
But you want to
colonize Mars and have

00:46:31.990 --> 00:46:34.870
people live there
permanently, on a planet where

00:46:34.870 --> 00:46:37.520
you can't breathe the air?

00:46:37.520 --> 00:46:39.760
People say, well,
we had explorers

00:46:39.760 --> 00:46:42.490
from the 15th
century who went out

00:46:42.490 --> 00:46:43.990
not knowing where they'll land.

00:46:43.990 --> 00:46:44.560
Sure.

00:46:44.560 --> 00:46:47.620
Columbus visits the New World.

00:46:47.620 --> 00:46:50.310
He gets off his ship.

00:46:50.310 --> 00:46:52.320
He can breathe the air.

00:46:52.320 --> 00:46:56.400
Other human beings are
there to greet him.

00:46:56.400 --> 00:46:59.220
He can eat the fruit
growing on the trees.

00:46:59.220 --> 00:47:02.770
And the trees are made of wood,
just like the trees in Europe.

00:47:02.770 --> 00:47:06.690
If his ship breaks,
he can mend his ship.

00:47:06.690 --> 00:47:10.590
If you crash land on Mars,
you can't go to the Home Depot

00:47:10.590 --> 00:47:11.570
and get a new--

00:47:11.570 --> 00:47:12.840
get pliers.

00:47:12.840 --> 00:47:15.960
You can't get
anything, let alone not

00:47:15.960 --> 00:47:17.440
being able to breathe the air.

00:47:17.440 --> 00:47:20.610
So in order to really
become a two-planet species,

00:47:20.610 --> 00:47:23.190
we have to terraform Mars.

00:47:23.190 --> 00:47:24.420
terraform it.

00:47:24.420 --> 00:47:26.260
I love me some
terraforming, don't get me--

00:47:26.260 --> 00:47:27.030
I love it.

00:47:27.030 --> 00:47:28.440
OK, great idea.

00:47:28.440 --> 00:47:31.470
So you seed it with microbes
and certain chemistries,

00:47:31.470 --> 00:47:37.290
that then operate on
their own chemical--

00:47:37.290 --> 00:47:41.100
by their own chemical forces, to
generate oxygen. And all this.

00:47:41.100 --> 00:47:41.850
OK.

00:47:41.850 --> 00:47:45.030
So now we figure out
how to terraform Mars.

00:47:45.030 --> 00:47:47.370
It's a stretch.

00:47:47.370 --> 00:47:49.200
But it's not out of reach.

00:47:49.200 --> 00:47:51.090
It's not out of our imagination.

00:47:51.090 --> 00:47:52.380
Terraform Mars.

00:47:52.380 --> 00:47:55.230
Now, we ship a
billion people there.

00:47:55.230 --> 00:47:58.830
Now, we have our eggs in
two baskets instead of one.

00:47:58.830 --> 00:48:00.780
Now, here's my issue.

00:48:00.780 --> 00:48:04.150
Are you worried about
a killer asteroid?

00:48:04.150 --> 00:48:08.010
It seems to me that
whatever effort

00:48:08.010 --> 00:48:12.870
it took to terraform Mars
and ship a billion people

00:48:12.870 --> 00:48:16.860
there is greater
than whatever effort

00:48:16.860 --> 00:48:19.380
it would take to
deflect the asteroid.

00:48:19.380 --> 00:48:20.880
I'm just thinking.

00:48:20.880 --> 00:48:21.810
A asteroid is coming.

00:48:21.810 --> 00:48:22.590
Let's deflect it.

00:48:22.590 --> 00:48:25.080
No, first, left
terraform Mars and ship

00:48:25.080 --> 00:48:26.200
a billion people there.

00:48:26.200 --> 00:48:27.608
And then everyone here dies.

00:48:27.608 --> 00:48:28.650
So that they've survived.

00:48:28.650 --> 00:48:31.920
No, let's deflect the asteroid.

00:48:31.920 --> 00:48:32.700
What else?

00:48:32.700 --> 00:48:35.940
There's a killer virus
that goes around.

00:48:35.940 --> 00:48:40.410
What investments will be
required in virology to create

00:48:40.410 --> 00:48:43.320
a perfect antiviral serum?

00:48:43.320 --> 00:48:44.160
Is that in reach?

00:48:44.160 --> 00:48:45.180
I don't know.

00:48:45.180 --> 00:48:49.410
I'm guessing it's less money,
less time, and less energy

00:48:49.410 --> 00:48:52.260
to terraform Mars and ship
a billion people there.

00:48:52.260 --> 00:48:56.700
And, finally, here we are,
we're trashing this planet.

00:48:56.700 --> 00:48:58.830
Climate change
makes it unlivable.

00:48:58.830 --> 00:49:00.370
Oh, my gosh.

00:49:00.370 --> 00:49:02.340
What do we do?

00:49:02.340 --> 00:49:06.180
So we terraform Mars and
ship a billion people there.

00:49:06.180 --> 00:49:08.500
But wait a minute.

00:49:08.500 --> 00:49:12.070
If we have the power
of geoengineering,

00:49:12.070 --> 00:49:15.930
to turn Mars into
Earth, then we have

00:49:15.930 --> 00:49:20.550
the power of geoengineering
to turn us back into Earth.

00:49:20.550 --> 00:49:23.250
So all of these
arguments, I don't

00:49:23.250 --> 00:49:29.470
see them having practical
meaning as a solution.

00:49:29.470 --> 00:49:29.970
No.

00:49:29.970 --> 00:49:31.860
I want to be a
two-planet species.

00:49:31.860 --> 00:49:34.890
But the motivation shouldn't
be, so that half of us

00:49:34.890 --> 00:49:35.570
can go there.

00:49:35.570 --> 00:49:37.800
So that one of these
planets could go extinct.

00:49:37.800 --> 00:49:38.680
No.

00:49:38.680 --> 00:49:39.180
No.

00:49:39.180 --> 00:49:41.340
Save everybody.

00:49:41.340 --> 00:49:43.110
Let's save every one.

00:49:43.110 --> 00:49:44.873
Is that so wrong?

00:49:44.873 --> 00:49:46.290
Let's just be a
two-planet species

00:49:46.290 --> 00:49:51.030
because that's fun to do,
because that would be great.

00:49:51.030 --> 00:49:53.250
That would be my
reason for doing it,

00:49:53.250 --> 00:49:59.370
not as the imagined excuse
to save the species.

00:49:59.370 --> 00:50:01.740
Speaking of climate
Armageddon, what

00:50:01.740 --> 00:50:03.730
do you think we need to
do to save the planet?

00:50:03.730 --> 00:50:04.230
Yeah.

00:50:04.230 --> 00:50:07.650
So Armageddon-- when
I think of Armageddon,

00:50:07.650 --> 00:50:10.140
I think of something more
catastrophic than what climate

00:50:10.140 --> 00:50:12.720
change will bring to us.

00:50:12.720 --> 00:50:18.630
So we are putting carbon
that has been long buried--

00:50:18.630 --> 00:50:21.960
long buried in the
ground, turning that

00:50:21.960 --> 00:50:24.900
into carbon dioxide
as the product

00:50:24.900 --> 00:50:28.660
of burning fossil fuels.

00:50:28.660 --> 00:50:30.780
So we are warming the Earth.

00:50:30.780 --> 00:50:33.540
This will have
catastrophic consequences

00:50:33.540 --> 00:50:40.320
to coastlines as we melt
the land-based glaciers.

00:50:40.320 --> 00:50:43.050
And all that water
flows into the oceans.

00:50:43.050 --> 00:50:45.210
And the ocean levels rise.

00:50:45.210 --> 00:50:47.340
I live in New York City.

00:50:47.340 --> 00:50:49.560
You can do the calculation.

00:50:49.560 --> 00:50:54.120
If Greenland loses its ice,
and Antarctica loses its ice,

00:50:54.120 --> 00:50:59.340
the water level will
rise to the left elbow

00:50:59.340 --> 00:51:01.940
of the Statue of Liberty.

00:51:01.940 --> 00:51:04.490
So basically, all of New
York City is under water.

00:51:04.490 --> 00:51:06.740
And so is basically
every other major city

00:51:06.740 --> 00:51:09.410
we've ever built in the
history of civilization

00:51:09.410 --> 00:51:12.200
because cities were built where
there was commerce, where there

00:51:12.200 --> 00:51:14.600
was transportation, where
there was-- and where's that?

00:51:14.600 --> 00:51:16.920
That's on the water's edge.

00:51:16.920 --> 00:51:21.900
So if all of what
founded civilization

00:51:21.900 --> 00:51:26.460
is put in threat because of
these rising tide waters,

00:51:26.460 --> 00:51:31.360
then, yeah, that's a
really bad future for us.

00:51:31.360 --> 00:51:33.670
But we will survive
it as a species.

00:51:33.670 --> 00:51:37.020
There's not-- it's
not an Armageddon.

00:51:37.020 --> 00:51:38.700
It's a slow burn.

00:51:38.700 --> 00:51:39.960
Actually, it's a fast burn.

00:51:39.960 --> 00:51:42.720
And we're not going to be able
to respond nimbly enough to it.

00:51:42.720 --> 00:51:44.460
We have to pick up
every coastal city

00:51:44.460 --> 00:51:47.610
and move it inland a
hundred miles, or 50 miles.

00:51:47.610 --> 00:51:49.870
Can we do that over decades?

00:51:49.870 --> 00:51:50.370
No.

00:51:50.370 --> 00:51:53.820
Because these cities
took centuries

00:51:53.820 --> 00:52:00.030
to build, and to perfect, and
to develop, and to have them

00:52:00.030 --> 00:52:02.520
function in the ways
that civilization

00:52:02.520 --> 00:52:03.630
needs and requires.

00:52:06.840 --> 00:52:08.910
Now, the reason I say
this there was a day

00:52:08.910 --> 00:52:11.550
before we had fossil fuels, we--

00:52:11.550 --> 00:52:13.620
Earth.

00:52:13.620 --> 00:52:16.770
It's called the-- the
Carboniferous era of Earth,

00:52:16.770 --> 00:52:20.460
all of that carbon
was plant life.

00:52:20.460 --> 00:52:21.630
And Earth was hot.

00:52:21.630 --> 00:52:23.460
We didn't have polar ice caps.

00:52:23.460 --> 00:52:25.830
Life was doing just fine.

00:52:25.830 --> 00:52:28.200
But humans had not
yet come along.

00:52:28.200 --> 00:52:30.000
And humans had not
yet built cities

00:52:30.000 --> 00:52:34.890
dependent on what we presumed
would be a stable climate.

00:52:34.890 --> 00:52:38.470
So Earth will definitely
survive anything we do to it.

00:52:38.470 --> 00:52:42.060
But our civilization, as we've
come to define it, will not.

00:52:42.060 --> 00:52:43.620
We can call that Armageddon.

00:52:43.620 --> 00:52:46.500
But there will be still
people who will survive.

00:52:46.500 --> 00:52:51.690
But it'll be a really
ugly world at that point.

00:52:51.690 --> 00:52:54.370
Neil, we are coming up
to the top of the hour.

00:52:54.370 --> 00:52:57.590
So let me ask you
one last question.

00:52:57.590 --> 00:52:58.590
Well, ask two questions.

00:52:58.590 --> 00:53:00.105
I'll answer these
in half the time.

00:53:00.105 --> 00:53:00.730
How about that?

00:53:00.730 --> 00:53:03.000
Last two questions, OK.

00:53:03.000 --> 00:53:11.550
So what do you see as a
landing on the Moon moment

00:53:11.550 --> 00:53:13.860
for space-time,
something that transcends

00:53:13.860 --> 00:53:18.060
national interests, something
that can unify the world?

00:53:18.060 --> 00:53:20.820
Could unify the world?

00:53:20.820 --> 00:53:25.440
A virus that can attack
any and all humans,

00:53:25.440 --> 00:53:27.900
and would be a
pandemic, and we all

00:53:27.900 --> 00:53:29.880
have to band
together to fight it.

00:53:29.880 --> 00:53:31.650
Oh, that already happened.

00:53:31.650 --> 00:53:32.850
And we didn't band together.

00:53:32.850 --> 00:53:36.000
Oh, silly me.

00:53:36.000 --> 00:53:38.520
Generally, people
band together when

00:53:38.520 --> 00:53:41.460
they have a common
need and a common urge.

00:53:41.460 --> 00:53:45.870
And the COVID virus
was a practice

00:53:45.870 --> 00:53:48.240
run for invading aliens.

00:53:48.240 --> 00:53:50.500
If an invading alien
wants to kill all humans.

00:53:50.500 --> 00:53:52.530
It's time to get together folks.

00:53:52.530 --> 00:53:54.000
Pool our resources.

00:53:54.000 --> 00:53:56.445
Put aside your tribal ways.

00:53:56.445 --> 00:53:58.470
In fact, let's
collect your tribalism

00:53:58.470 --> 00:54:01.200
into one sense of the world.

00:54:01.200 --> 00:54:03.330
And that one sense of
the world is that--

00:54:03.330 --> 00:54:04.260
what race are you?

00:54:04.260 --> 00:54:06.552
We are all the human race.

00:54:06.552 --> 00:54:08.010
Now, let's fight
this common enemy.

00:54:08.010 --> 00:54:09.390
We failed that.

00:54:09.390 --> 00:54:13.170
The only success story was
the medical researchers

00:54:13.170 --> 00:54:18.000
who developed the virus,
that the antiviral vaccine,

00:54:18.000 --> 00:54:20.160
in record time.

00:54:20.160 --> 00:54:23.550
That's why I wouldn't give it
quite an F of a COVID year.

00:54:23.550 --> 00:54:25.380
I'd give it like a C plus.

00:54:25.380 --> 00:54:27.480
And the fact that
it's not an F is

00:54:27.480 --> 00:54:29.370
because we got
the vaccine going,

00:54:29.370 --> 00:54:31.650
even though the
distribution had problems.

00:54:31.650 --> 00:54:38.250
So why should we need a
Sputnik moment or Moon moment?

00:54:38.250 --> 00:54:39.720
Why?

00:54:39.720 --> 00:54:41.490
We know we all enjoyed it.

00:54:41.490 --> 00:54:45.570
We know it transformed our
culture, our civilization,

00:54:45.570 --> 00:54:46.950
our technologies.

00:54:46.950 --> 00:54:49.350
It stoked our economies.

00:54:49.350 --> 00:54:51.710
We know this.

00:54:51.710 --> 00:54:55.900
Why do we have to feel
threatened again just to engage

00:54:55.900 --> 00:54:58.960
in exploration on the frontier.

00:54:58.960 --> 00:55:02.140
Knowing from past experience
that such exploration

00:55:02.140 --> 00:55:06.020
can be transformative
to how we live?

00:55:06.020 --> 00:55:11.180
So, yeah, that's my
answer to that question.

00:55:11.180 --> 00:55:12.280
Thank you for that.

00:55:12.280 --> 00:55:13.170
OK, last question.

00:55:13.170 --> 00:55:13.670
Yeah.

00:55:13.670 --> 00:55:17.950
If you could go back to
your 21-year-old self, which

00:55:17.950 --> 00:55:21.010
is the age of many of the
students who are with us today,

00:55:21.010 --> 00:55:22.450
what advice would you give?

00:55:25.420 --> 00:55:30.220
I have what might be an unfair
satisfying answer for you.

00:55:34.410 --> 00:55:39.270
Everything I am today,
everything, down to the tiniest

00:55:39.270 --> 00:55:49.990
detail, is what it is
because throughout your life

00:55:49.990 --> 00:55:51.143
you test things.

00:55:51.143 --> 00:55:52.060
You experiment things.

00:55:52.060 --> 00:55:52.990
And you learn.

00:55:52.990 --> 00:55:56.140
You learn to do things
better-- or this didn't work.

00:55:56.140 --> 00:55:58.400
And you learned this.

00:55:58.400 --> 00:56:00.563
If someone goes back
in time and says

00:56:00.563 --> 00:56:02.230
don't do that, that's
not going to work,

00:56:02.230 --> 00:56:04.810
and they just tell
you, then that's not

00:56:04.810 --> 00:56:06.490
part of your life experience.

00:56:06.490 --> 00:56:10.370
That's just somebody handing
you their life experience.

00:56:10.370 --> 00:56:16.660
So when I think of advice,
the advice is keep exploring.

00:56:16.660 --> 00:56:18.400
Just keep doing
what you're doing.

00:56:18.400 --> 00:56:19.990
And, yeah, there'll be
some failures in there.

00:56:19.990 --> 00:56:20.990
You'll learn from those.

00:56:20.990 --> 00:56:22.450
There'll be some successes.

00:56:22.450 --> 00:56:24.140
You'll do more of that.

00:56:24.140 --> 00:56:26.830
But you will have
experienced that on your own

00:56:26.830 --> 00:56:30.310
as the consequence of
your own creativity.

00:56:30.310 --> 00:56:33.250
And so it reminds me--

00:56:33.250 --> 00:56:35.840
it's now-- it's an
apocryphal story.

00:56:35.840 --> 00:56:39.310
But let me just
tell such a story.

00:56:39.310 --> 00:56:42.280
So you have an immigrant who
comes to the United States

00:56:42.280 --> 00:56:45.370
with $20 in their pocket.

00:56:45.370 --> 00:56:46.360
Then they work hard.

00:56:46.360 --> 00:56:47.260
And they struggle.

00:56:47.260 --> 00:56:49.480
And they take odd jobs.

00:56:49.480 --> 00:56:52.668
And then they finally
own a business

00:56:52.668 --> 00:56:53.710
because they worked hard.

00:56:53.710 --> 00:56:54.640
And they worked their way up.

00:56:54.640 --> 00:56:56.020
And they had more-- they saved.

00:56:56.020 --> 00:56:57.160
They own a business.

00:56:57.160 --> 00:56:58.270
They get married.

00:56:58.270 --> 00:56:59.500
They have children.

00:56:59.500 --> 00:57:01.240
And then they have
multiple businesses.

00:57:01.240 --> 00:57:02.920
And they become
really successful.

00:57:02.920 --> 00:57:05.020
And it lives in a whole mansion.

00:57:05.020 --> 00:57:08.140
But that person
struggled his entire life

00:57:08.140 --> 00:57:12.670
trying to make ends meet, barely
getting food on the plate.

00:57:12.670 --> 00:57:14.230
So now they're well off.

00:57:14.230 --> 00:57:15.850
And they have kids.

00:57:15.850 --> 00:57:19.960
And they make sure
that the kids do not

00:57:19.960 --> 00:57:22.757
struggle the way they did.

00:57:22.757 --> 00:57:25.090
They make sure the kids always
have food on their plate,

00:57:25.090 --> 00:57:28.090
the way they did because I
don't want my kids to struggle

00:57:28.090 --> 00:57:28.780
the way I did.

00:57:28.780 --> 00:57:30.220
And then the kids grow up.

00:57:30.220 --> 00:57:32.080
And they're 21 years old.

00:57:32.080 --> 00:57:34.510
And they're deadbeats.

00:57:34.510 --> 00:57:39.120
And then the parents say,
where did we go wrong?

00:57:39.120 --> 00:57:44.550
We gave you everything
we didn't have.

00:57:44.550 --> 00:57:46.830
That's where you went wrong.

00:57:46.830 --> 00:57:51.390
Because you became what you
were because of the challenges

00:57:51.390 --> 00:57:54.270
and struggles that you
faced in your life.

00:57:54.270 --> 00:57:59.910
I don't-- I think we undervalue
struggle and what role it can

00:57:59.910 --> 00:58:04.030
play in your future success,
in your future perspectives,

00:58:04.030 --> 00:58:09.410
your outlooks, your
sense of the world,

00:58:09.410 --> 00:58:10.900
especially since almost--

00:58:10.900 --> 00:58:15.960
hardly anything worth
achieving will you end up

00:58:15.960 --> 00:58:17.990
achieving without a struggle.

00:58:17.990 --> 00:58:19.990
And if someone said here,
just do that, then you

00:58:19.990 --> 00:58:20.948
don't have to struggle.

00:58:20.948 --> 00:58:22.900
Well, OK, you made
your life easier.

00:58:22.900 --> 00:58:25.690
Is that really what--

00:58:25.690 --> 00:58:27.430
you just want an easy life?

00:58:27.430 --> 00:58:28.600
Really?

00:58:28.600 --> 00:58:29.560
OK.

00:58:29.560 --> 00:58:30.620
Go ahead and get it.

00:58:30.620 --> 00:58:32.032
You take your easy life.

00:58:32.032 --> 00:58:33.490
What's going to
happen when there's

00:58:33.490 --> 00:58:36.530
some stress in your life
or you'll freak out?

00:58:36.530 --> 00:58:37.900
You won't know how to handle it.

00:58:37.900 --> 00:58:41.470
You'll-- so I don't know.

00:58:41.470 --> 00:58:43.570
I don't want to play
psychologist here.

00:58:43.570 --> 00:58:48.190
But there are people who
are having trouble in school

00:58:48.190 --> 00:58:51.250
because they're not
socialized in the right way.

00:58:51.250 --> 00:58:52.550
And they're getting bullied.

00:58:52.550 --> 00:58:53.880
So they take them
out of the school.

00:58:53.880 --> 00:58:54.880
They put them in a
different school,

00:58:54.880 --> 00:58:56.622
where everyone is
nice to each other.

00:58:56.622 --> 00:58:58.330
And then when they go
get older, and they

00:58:58.330 --> 00:59:00.040
get into the
workplace-- and then

00:59:00.040 --> 00:59:02.860
there's the difficult
person in the other cubicle.

00:59:02.860 --> 00:59:04.900
They don't know how to handle.

00:59:04.900 --> 00:59:08.290
I keep thinking so much
of what also goes on

00:59:08.290 --> 00:59:13.530
in school, beyond the
classwork, is learning how

00:59:13.530 --> 00:59:16.480
to deal with difficult people.

00:59:16.480 --> 00:59:20.940
And so, yeah-- so in terms
of advice, no, I wouldn't--

00:59:20.940 --> 00:59:23.160
I don't know that I have advice.

00:59:23.160 --> 00:59:27.510
Suppose I said, spend less time
doing athletics and more time--

00:59:27.510 --> 00:59:29.970
because I did a lot of
athletics-- and more time

00:59:29.970 --> 00:59:32.130
in your studies.

00:59:32.130 --> 00:59:34.362
Maybe I could have done that.

00:59:34.362 --> 00:59:36.570
But then I wouldn't have
this appreciation for sports

00:59:36.570 --> 00:59:37.290
that I currently do.

00:59:37.290 --> 00:59:38.832
And I wouldn't have
the conversations

00:59:38.832 --> 00:59:40.500
that I have with
sports enthusiasts.

00:59:40.500 --> 00:59:43.200
And I'm actually
like friends with--

00:59:43.200 --> 00:59:46.050
well, strong acquaintances--
with the coach

00:59:46.050 --> 00:59:48.300
of the Seattle Seahawks.

00:59:48.300 --> 00:59:49.950
That's just fun.

00:59:49.950 --> 00:59:51.870
and I have a fluency
in sports that

00:59:51.870 --> 00:59:54.690
has enriched my life in ways
that wouldn't have been there

00:59:54.690 --> 00:59:56.940
if I gave up all my
sports at the time.

00:59:56.940 --> 00:59:59.760
And then I only had
my head in the books.

00:59:59.760 --> 01:00:00.908
I used to--

01:00:00.908 --> 01:00:01.950
I didn't doodle in class.

01:00:01.950 --> 01:00:03.158
I don't have any art ability.

01:00:03.158 --> 01:00:06.750
But I would play with my pen as
though I were a calligrapher.

01:00:06.750 --> 01:00:10.140
And I would do fancy lettering.

01:00:10.140 --> 01:00:12.690
And it turns out that The New
York Times, the banner head,

01:00:12.690 --> 01:00:14.310
it's in Gothic.

01:00:14.310 --> 01:00:17.560
A lot of the letters in The New
York Times appear in my name.

01:00:17.560 --> 01:00:18.810
So I'd pull out those letters.

01:00:18.810 --> 01:00:19.800
And I do them.

01:00:19.800 --> 01:00:21.750
And there I am, like
I really should have

01:00:21.750 --> 01:00:23.340
been paying attention in class.

01:00:23.340 --> 01:00:25.290
And how does this accrue at all?

01:00:25.290 --> 01:00:27.750
Well, I now have a
collection of fountain pens.

01:00:27.750 --> 01:00:31.320
And I dip them in
ink, in ink bottles.

01:00:31.320 --> 01:00:34.560
And when I signed my
books, I signed them

01:00:34.560 --> 01:00:35.730
with a calligraphy pen.

01:00:35.730 --> 01:00:39.240
And people like--
they like that.

01:00:39.240 --> 01:00:39.930
I do that.

01:00:39.930 --> 01:00:41.670
And it gets a gets
on the doorstep.

01:00:41.670 --> 01:00:46.020
And it shows up on social
media, a little thing like that.

01:00:46.020 --> 01:00:47.220
That's enriched my life.

01:00:47.220 --> 01:00:49.330
It's enriched the
lives of others.

01:00:49.330 --> 01:00:53.950
So don't tell me, future person,
what I should or shouldn't

01:00:53.950 --> 01:00:54.450
be doing.

01:00:54.450 --> 01:00:57.330
I'm perfectly
happy to experiment

01:00:57.330 --> 01:01:00.270
and learn from it on my own.

01:01:00.270 --> 01:01:01.710
Thank you for that, Neil.

01:01:01.710 --> 01:01:03.040
Thank you for being with us.

01:01:03.040 --> 01:01:07.930
Thank you for inspiring
us and encouraging us.

01:01:07.930 --> 01:01:10.140
I look forward to
the next time you

01:01:10.140 --> 01:01:11.680
and I have a chance to meet.

01:01:11.680 --> 01:01:13.470
In the meantime,
we'll work really hard

01:01:13.470 --> 01:01:15.210
on those self-driving cars.

01:01:15.210 --> 01:01:15.935
Yes.

01:01:15.935 --> 01:01:20.580
And, in fact, I want to get
you on my podcast, StarTalk.

01:01:20.580 --> 01:01:24.000
The day the first self-driving
car, fully self-driving,

01:01:24.000 --> 01:01:28.800
drives off the lot I want you
in my studio, to interview you.

01:01:28.800 --> 01:01:31.080
Oh, one thing about your--

01:01:31.080 --> 01:01:33.060
here's the advice to
give my 21-year-old self.

01:01:33.060 --> 01:01:33.816
You ready?

01:01:33.816 --> 01:01:35.520
I'm ready.

01:01:35.520 --> 01:01:40.480
Make sure you actually do
learn from your mistakes.

01:01:40.480 --> 01:01:42.150
That's the advice.

01:01:42.150 --> 01:01:44.790
You don't want to ever make
the same mistake twice,

01:01:44.790 --> 01:01:48.110
wasting your life, and
time, and everybody else's.

01:01:48.110 --> 01:01:49.120
Awesome advice.

01:01:49.120 --> 01:01:50.105
Thank you, Neil.

01:01:50.105 --> 01:01:51.780
And Danielle, always
good to see you.

01:01:51.780 --> 01:01:54.270
And I love you've got the
Horsehead Nebula behind you,

01:01:54.270 --> 01:01:55.808
a little bit of
our universe there.

01:01:55.808 --> 01:01:56.850
There's the horse's head.

01:01:56.850 --> 01:01:58.295
You're blocking--
you're blocking

01:01:58.295 --> 01:01:59.670
the thing that
gives it its name,

01:01:59.670 --> 01:02:01.640
I just want you to know that.

01:02:01.640 --> 01:02:03.270
That was a test for you.

01:02:03.270 --> 01:02:06.940
OK, I know-- I know my sky.

01:02:06.940 --> 01:02:09.780
Who do think you're
talking to here?

01:02:09.780 --> 01:02:12.990
It was taken in my backyard.

01:02:12.990 --> 01:02:13.650
Oh, it was.

01:02:13.650 --> 01:02:16.860
Excellent, excellent.

01:02:16.860 --> 01:02:17.430
So was this.

01:02:17.430 --> 01:02:19.536
This was taken in my backyard.

01:02:19.536 --> 01:02:21.408
[LAUGHTER]

01:02:23.280 --> 01:02:25.120
Well, thank you
all for joining us.

01:02:25.120 --> 01:02:25.980
Thank you, Neil.

01:02:25.980 --> 01:02:29.850
OK, and everyone in MIT land,
stay strong, stay smart.

01:02:29.850 --> 01:02:34.620
And you all know
that you're going

01:02:34.620 --> 01:02:36.150
to become the
engines of tomorrow's

01:02:36.150 --> 01:02:38.590
economies and tomorrow's
science literacy.

01:02:38.590 --> 01:02:40.860
Just keep it going.

01:02:40.860 --> 01:02:43.010
Absolutely.

